var e=[{topicId:`pm-basics-staining`,level:`basic`,blocks:[{kind:`para`,text:`Pharmaceutical microbiology starts with knowing what a bacterium is made of, how to make it visible, and how to grow it. A bacterium is a prokaryote: a single cell with no true nucleus and no membrane-bound organelles. Everything else in this topic follows from that.`},{kind:`heading`,text:`Prokaryote versus eukaryote`},{kind:`table`,headers:[`Feature`,`Prokaryote (bacteria)`,`Eukaryote (fungi, protozoa)`],rows:[[`Nucleus`,`Nucleoid, no nuclear membrane`,`True nucleus with membrane`],[`Size`,`0.2 to 2 micrometre`,`10 to 100 micrometre`],[`Ribosome`,`70S (50S + 30S)`,`80S (60S + 40S)`],[`DNA`,`Single circular chromosome, plasmids`,`Several linear chromosomes with histones`],[`Organelles`,`Absent (no mitochondria)`,`Mitochondria, ER, Golgi present`],[`Cell wall`,`Peptidoglycan (murein)`,`Chitin in fungi, cellulose in plants`],[`Membrane sterols`,`Absent except Mycoplasma`,`Present (ergosterol, cholesterol)`],[`Division`,`Binary fission`,`Mitosis and meiosis`]],caption:`The 70S versus 80S difference is why aminoglycosides, tetracyclines and macrolides are selectively toxic to bacteria.`},{kind:`heading`,text:`Parts of a bacterial cell`},{kind:`bullets`,items:[`Capsule: outer polysaccharide layer, resists phagocytosis, makes the colony mucoid.`,`Cell wall: peptidoglycan mesh that gives shape and resists osmotic lysis.`,`Plasma membrane: lipid bilayer carrying respiratory enzymes, since there are no mitochondria.`,`Mesosome: infolding of the membrane, involved in septum formation and respiration.`,`Nucleoid: the naked circular chromosome; plasmids are small extra circles carrying resistance genes.`,`Ribosomes: 70S, the site of protein synthesis and the target of many antibiotics.`,`Inclusion bodies: food stores such as volutin (metachromatic) granules in Corynebacterium diphtheriae.`,`Endospore: a dormant heat-resistant body made by Bacillus and Clostridium only.`,`Flagella: for motility; pili or fimbriae for sticking to surfaces; sex pilus for conjugation.`]},{kind:`callout`,tone:`mnemonic`,title:`Flagellar arrangements`,text:`Mono = one at one end; Amphi = one at each end; Lopho = a tuft at one end; Peri = all over the cell (as in Salmonella and E. coli).`},{kind:`heading`,text:`Staining: why it is needed`},{kind:`para`,text:`Bacteria are colourless and almost invisible under an ordinary light microscope. A dye with a charged, coloured ion (a chromophore) is used to give contrast. Basic dyes such as crystal violet, methylene blue and safranin carry a positive charge and bind the negatively charged bacterial surface, so they stain the cell. Acidic dyes such as nigrosin and India ink are repelled by the cell and stain only the background, which is called negative staining.`},{kind:`numbered`,items:[`Smear: spread the culture thinly on a clean slide.`,`Air dry.`,`Heat fix: pass through a flame to stick the cells down and kill them.`,`Apply stain or stains in sequence.`,`Wash, blot dry, examine under oil immersion (100x).`]},{kind:`callout`,tone:`exam`,title:`Gram stain in one line`,text:`Gram positive cells hold crystal violet and appear purple; Gram negative cells lose it during decolourisation and take up the safranin counterstain, appearing pink or red.`},{kind:`heading`,text:`Growing bacteria`},{kind:`para`,text:`A culture medium supplies water, carbon, nitrogen, minerals and growth factors. Agar, obtained from seaweed, is the standard solidifying agent: it melts near 95 to 100 degrees C, sets around 40 to 42 degrees C, and almost no bacterium can digest it. Solid media use 1.5 to 2 percent agar, semisolid media about 0.5 percent, and broth uses none.`}]},{topicId:`pm-basics-staining`,level:`intermediate`,blocks:[{kind:`para`,text:`At exam level you must be able to reproduce the Gram positive versus Gram negative wall comparison, list every staining sequence with its reagents in the correct order, name the growth curve phases, and classify culture media by purpose.`},{kind:`heading`,text:`Cell wall comparison`},{kind:`table`,headers:[`Feature`,`Gram positive`,`Gram negative`],rows:[[`Peptidoglycan`,`Thick, 20 to 80 nm, 40 to 90 percent of wall`,`Thin, 2 to 7 nm, 5 to 20 percent`],[`Teichoic acid`,`Present`,`Absent`],[`Outer membrane`,`Absent`,`Present, with porins`],[`Lipopolysaccharide`,`Absent`,`Present (endotoxin, lipid A is the toxic part)`],[`Lipid content of wall`,`Low, 2 to 5 percent`,`High, 15 to 20 percent`],[`Periplasmic space`,`Virtually absent`,`Well developed, holds beta-lactamase`],[`Lysozyme`,`Highly sensitive`,`Relatively resistant`],[`Penicillin G`,`Sensitive`,`Generally resistant (poor penetration)`],[`Main toxin`,`Exotoxin (protein, secreted)`,`Endotoxin (LPS, released on lysis)`],[`Wall-less form`,`Protoplast (complete wall loss)`,`Spheroplast (partial wall loss)`]],caption:`Endotoxin equals lipopolysaccharide equals pyrogen: the reason parenteral products are tested for bacterial endotoxin.`},{kind:`heading`,text:`Why the Gram stain works`},{kind:`numbered`,items:[`Crystal violet (primary stain, about 1 min) enters both cell types.`,`Grams iodine (mordant, about 1 min) forms a large insoluble crystal violet-iodine complex inside the cell.`,`Ethanol or acetone-alcohol (decolouriser, 10 to 30 s) dehydrates and shrinks the thick Gram positive peptidoglycan, closing its pores and trapping the complex; in Gram negatives it dissolves the lipid-rich outer membrane and the thin peptidoglycan cannot hold the complex, which washes out.`,`Safranin or dilute carbol fuchsin (counterstain, 30 to 60 s) colours the now-colourless Gram negative cells pink.`]},{kind:`callout`,tone:`trap`,title:`The step that decides everything`,text:`Decolourisation is the critical and most error-prone step. Over-decolourisation makes Gram positives look Gram negative; under-decolourisation makes Gram negatives look Gram positive. Old cultures of Gram positives also decolourise easily, so always Gram stain a young culture (18 to 24 h).`},{kind:`heading`,text:`The other staining methods`},{kind:`table`,headers:[`Method`,`Reagents in order`,`Result`],rows:[[`Gram`,`Crystal violet, iodine, alcohol, safranin`,`G+ purple, G- pink`],[`Acid-fast (Ziehl-Neelsen)`,`Carbol fuchsin with heat, acid-alcohol, methylene blue`,`Acid-fast red, others blue`],[`Negative`,`India ink or nigrosin only, no heat fixing`,`Clear cell on dark background`],[`Spore (Schaeffer-Fulton)`,`Malachite green with steam, water wash, safranin`,`Spore green, cell red`],[`Capsule (Anthonys)`,`Crystal violet, then 20 percent copper sulphate`,`Halo around a purple cell`],[`Flagella (Leifson)`,`Tannic acid mordant, then basic fuchsin`,`Thickened, visible flagella`]],caption:`Only two of these use heat during staining: Ziehl-Neelsen and the malachite green spore stain.`},{kind:`para`,text:`Acid-fastness comes from mycolic acid, a long-chain fatty acid in the wall of Mycobacterium and Nocardia. It makes the wall waxy, so carbol fuchsin has to be driven in with heat, and once in it resists decolourisation by 20 percent sulphuric acid or 3 percent acid-alcohol. Mycobacterium tuberculosis is stained with 20 percent sulphuric acid, Mycobacterium leprae with the milder 5 percent.`},{kind:`heading`,text:`Bacterial growth curve`},{kind:`table`,headers:[`Phase`,`What happens`,`Note`],rows:[[`Lag`,`No division, cells enlarge and make enzymes`,`Cells are most susceptible to heat here`],[`Log (exponential)`,`Constant maximum growth rate, straight line on log plot`,`Cells most uniform; used for antibiotic testing`],[`Stationary`,`Growth rate equals death rate, nutrients low, toxins accumulate`,`Spores and most secondary metabolites appear`],[`Decline (death)`,`Death exceeds division, logarithmic fall in viable count`,`Involution forms seen`]]},{kind:`formula`,expr:`n = (log Nt - log N0) / log 2 = 3.3 (log Nt - log N0);  g = t / n`,caption:`n = number of generations, Nt = final count, N0 = initial count, t = time elapsed, g = generation (doubling) time. E. coli g is about 20 min, M. tuberculosis about 18 to 20 h.`},{kind:`heading`,text:`Classification of culture media`},{kind:`table`,headers:[`Type`,`Purpose`,`Example`],rows:[[`Basal (simple)`,`Routine growth`,`Nutrient broth and nutrient agar`],[`Enriched`,`Add nutrients for fastidious organisms`,`Blood agar, chocolate agar`],[`Enrichment (liquid)`,`Favour one organism, suppress others`,`Selenite F broth, tetrathionate broth`],[`Selective (solid)`,`Inhibitors let only the target grow`,`MacConkey, DCA, TCBS, Lowenstein-Jensen`],[`Differential`,`Distinguish colonies by appearance`,`MacConkey: lactose fermenters pink`],[`Transport`,`Keep organisms alive without multiplying`,`Stuarts, Cary-Blair, VR medium`],[`Anaerobic`,`Remove or exclude oxygen`,`Robertsons cooked meat, thioglycollate`]]},{kind:`callout`,tone:`exam`,title:`Aseptic technique`,text:`Aseptic technique means preventing entry of contaminants: work in a laminar air flow cabinet supplied through a HEPA filter, flame the loop to red heat before and after use, disinfect surfaces with 70 percent isopropyl alcohol (not absolute alcohol, which fixes rather than kills), and never pass a hand over an open plate.`}]},{topicId:`pm-basics-staining`,level:`advanced`,blocks:[{kind:`para`,text:`The high-scoring detail here is peptidoglycan chemistry, the exceptions to the Gram rule, spore architecture and quantitative growth arithmetic.`},{kind:`heading`,text:`Peptidoglycan chemistry`},{kind:`bullets`,items:[`Backbone: alternating N-acetylglucosamine (NAG) and N-acetylmuramic acid (NAM) joined by beta-1,4 links.`,`A tetrapeptide hangs from each NAM: L-alanine, D-glutamate, then L-lysine (most Gram positives) or diaminopimelic acid (Gram negatives and Bacillus), then D-alanine-D-alanine.`,`Cross-link: transpeptidase (a penicillin-binding protein) joins the chains, releasing the terminal D-alanine. Staphylococcus aureus uses a pentaglycine bridge.`,`Penicillin is a structural analogue of D-alanyl-D-alanine, so it acylates and inactivates transpeptidase; the wall cannot cross-link and the cell lyses.`,`Lysozyme hydrolyses the beta-1,4 bond between NAM and NAG, cutting the backbone itself.`]},{kind:`callout`,tone:`trap`,title:`Antibiotics act only on growing cells`,text:`Cell-wall-active agents such as penicillins and cephalosporins are bactericidal only against actively dividing cells, which is why they work in the log phase and why they cannot kill dormant spores or non-growing persisters.`},{kind:`heading`,text:`Exceptions worth memorising`},{kind:`table`,headers:[`Organism`,`Peculiarity`,`Consequence`],rows:[[`Mycoplasma`,`No cell wall at all; sterols in membrane`,`Beta-lactam resistant; smallest free-living cell`],[`Mycobacterium`,`Mycolic acid, arabinogalactan, cord factor`,`Acid-fast, weakly Gram positive, slow growing`],[`Archaea`,`Pseudomurein, no true peptidoglycan`,`Lysozyme and penicillin insensitive`],[`L-forms`,`Wall-deficient variants of walled bacteria`,`Revert when the antibiotic is withdrawn`],[`Chlamydia`,`Obligate intracellular, no muramic acid`,`Cannot be grown on ordinary media`]]},{kind:`heading`,text:`Endospore architecture`},{kind:`bullets`,items:[`Core: dehydrated cytoplasm holding calcium dipicolinate, which is the main reason for heat resistance.`,`Cortex: loosely cross-linked peptidoglycan, maintains core dehydration.`,`Spore coat: keratin-like protein layers, gives chemical and radiation resistance.`,`Exosporium: an outer lipoprotein envelope, present in some species.`,`Position of the spore is a taxonomic clue: terminal and bulging (drumstick) in Clostridium tetani, subterminal in Clostridium botulinum, central in Bacillus anthracis.`]},{kind:`formula`,expr:`log Nt = log N0 + (t / g) x log 2`,caption:`A rearrangement of the generation-time equation. Example: 10^3 cells with g = 20 min grown for 4 h give n = 240/20 = 12 generations, so Nt = 10^3 x 2^12 = 4.096 x 10^6 cells.`},{kind:`table`,headers:[`Counting method`,`What it counts`,`Limitation`],rows:[[`Pour or spread plate`,`Viable cells (CFU)`,`Slow; clumps count as one`],[`Direct microscopic count`,`Total cells, live plus dead`,`Cannot distinguish viability`],[`Turbidity at 600 nm`,`Biomass, indirect`,`Needs a calibration curve`],[`Most probable number`,`Statistical viable estimate`,`Wide confidence limits`],[`Membrane filtration`,`Viable cells in large dilute volumes`,`Blocked by particulates`]],caption:`For pharmaceutical microbial limit testing the plate count in colony forming units per gram or millilitre is the reference method.`},{kind:`callout`,tone:`exam`,title:`Classic single-fact questions`,text:`Volutin (metachromatic, Babes-Ernst) granules stain with Alberts stain and are typical of Corynebacterium diphtheriae. Poly-beta-hydroxybutyrate is a lipid storage inclusion. Gas vacuoles occur in aquatic bacteria. Bacterial flagella are made of flagellin and rotate; eukaryotic flagella have a 9 + 2 microtubule pattern and beat.`}]},{topicId:`pm-sterilisation`,level:`basic`,blocks:[{kind:`para`,text:`Sterilisation is the complete destruction or removal of all viable microorganisms including bacterial spores. It is absolute: an article is either sterile or it is not. Disinfection only reduces harmful organisms on inanimate surfaces, and antisepsis does the same on living tissue.`},{kind:`bullets`,items:[`Sterilisation: kills or removes everything, spores included.`,`Disinfection: kills vegetative pathogens on objects; spores usually survive.`,`Antiseptic: a chemical safe enough to apply to skin or mucosa.`,`Sanitisation: reduces microbial numbers to a level judged safe by public health standards.`,`Sepsis versus asepsis: presence versus absence of contaminating organisms.`,`Bacteriostatic agents stop growth; bactericidal agents kill.`]},{kind:`heading`,text:`The five families of methods`},{kind:`numbered`,items:[`Moist heat: autoclaving with saturated steam under pressure.`,`Dry heat: hot air oven, flaming, incineration.`,`Filtration: physical removal of organisms, for heat-labile liquids and gases.`,`Gaseous: ethylene oxide, formaldehyde, hydrogen peroxide vapour.`,`Radiation: gamma rays and electron beam (sterilising), ultraviolet (surface only).`]},{kind:`callout`,tone:`exam`,title:`The single most asked autoclave fact`,text:`Standard autoclave cycle: 121 degrees C at 15 psi (about 1.05 kg per sq cm gauge) for 15 minutes with saturated steam.`},{kind:`para`,text:`Moist heat kills by coagulating and denaturing proteins; dry heat kills by oxidising cell constituents. Because water conducts heat far better and denaturation needs less energy than oxidation, moist heat works at a much lower temperature and in far less time than dry heat.`},{kind:`table`,headers:[`Method`,`Typical condition`,`Used for`],rows:[[`Autoclave`,`121 degrees C, 15 psi, 15 min`,`Aqueous injections, media, dressings`],[`Hot air oven`,`160 degrees C for 2 h`,`Glassware, powders, oils`],[`Membrane filter`,`0.22 micrometre pore`,`Heat-labile solutions, e.g. insulin`],[`Ethylene oxide`,`45 to 63 degrees C, several hours`,`Plastic devices, catheters, sutures`],[`Gamma radiation`,`25 kGy`,`Prepacked disposables, sutures`]]},{kind:`callout`,tone:`trap`,title:`Air is the enemy of the autoclave`,text:`Residual air in the chamber lowers the actual steam temperature even though the pressure gauge reads 15 psi. That is why air must be displaced downward or removed by a vacuum pump before the timed holding period begins.`},{kind:`para`,text:`Whenever the product can survive it, terminal sterilisation in the final sealed container is preferred, because every unit is treated. Only when the product or container cannot take the process is aseptic processing used, in which sterile components are combined without any final sterilisation step.`}]},{topicId:`pm-sterilisation`,level:`intermediate`,blocks:[{kind:`para`,text:`This is the highest-yield topic in pharmaceutical microbiology. Learn every time-temperature combination, the three kinetic values D, Z and F0, the pore sizes in filtration, and which method suits which product.`},{kind:`heading`,text:`Moist heat combinations`},{kind:`table`,headers:[`Temperature`,`Gauge pressure`,`Holding time`],rows:[[`115 degrees C`,`10 psi`,`30 min`],[`121 degrees C`,`15 psi`,`15 min`],[`126 degrees C`,`20 psi`,`10 min`],[`134 degrees C`,`32 psi`,`3 min`]],caption:`All four give an equivalent lethality. Times are holding times after the whole load has reached temperature.`},{kind:`bullets`,items:[`Tyndallisation (fractional sterilisation): 100 degrees C for 20 min on three successive days, with incubation between so that spores germinate and are killed on the next round.`,`Boiling at 100 degrees C for 10 to 20 min is disinfection, not sterilisation: spores survive.`,`Pasteurisation, holder method: 63 degrees C for 30 min. HTST flash method: 72 degrees C for 15 s. UHT: 140 degrees C for a few seconds.`,`Autoclave types: downward displacement (gravity) for unwrapped goods and fluids; pre-vacuum (porous load) for dressings; rotary for large-volume ampoules.`]},{kind:`heading`,text:`Dry heat combinations`},{kind:`table`,headers:[`Temperature`,`Holding time`,`Comment`],rows:[[`140 degrees C`,`180 min`,`Rarely used, very long`],[`150 degrees C`,`150 min`,`For thermolabile powders`],[`160 degrees C`,`120 min`,`Official reference cycle`],[`170 degrees C`,`60 min`,`Common in practice`],[`180 degrees C`,`30 min`,`Fastest routine oven cycle`],[`250 degrees C`,`30 min`,`Depyrogenation of glass, not just sterilisation`]],caption:`Depyrogenation destroys bacterial endotoxin; a 121 degrees C autoclave cycle sterilises but does not depyrogenate.`},{kind:`heading`,text:`The three kinetic values`},{kind:`formula`,expr:`D value = time in minutes at a stated temperature to kill 90 percent (one log) of the population`,caption:`Written D121 when measured at 121 degrees C. It is the reciprocal of the slope of the survivor curve.`},{kind:`formula`,expr:`log N0 - log Nt = t / D`,caption:`N0 = initial count, Nt = count after time t, D = D value. Rearranged: t = D x (log N0 - log Nt).`},{kind:`formula`,expr:`Z value = degrees C rise needed to reduce the D value tenfold`,caption:`For saturated steam Z is taken as 10 degrees C; for dry heat it is about 20 degrees C.`},{kind:`formula`,expr:`F0 = delta t x sum of 10 raised to ((T - 121) / 10)`,caption:`F0 = lethality of the whole cycle expressed as equivalent minutes at 121 degrees C, with Z = 10 degrees C. T = product temperature at each interval, delta t = length of each interval in minutes. Pharmacopoeial minimum F0 is 8 minutes; 12 is commonly targeted.`},{kind:`callout`,tone:`exam`,title:`Worked numerical 1`,text:`Bioburden 10^6 per container, D121 = 1.5 min, required sterility assurance level 10^-6. Total log reduction needed = 6 - (-6) = 12 logs. Time = 12 x 1.5 = 18 minutes at 121 degrees C.`},{kind:`callout`,tone:`exam`,title:`Worked numerical 2`,text:`A load holds at 115 degrees C for 30 min. F0 = 30 x 10^((115-121)/10) = 30 x 10^-0.6 = 30 x 0.251 = 7.5 minutes. That is below 8, so the cycle would fail a pharmacopoeial F0 requirement.`},{kind:`heading`,text:`Filtration`},{kind:`table`,headers:[`Pore size`,`Retains`,`Application`],rows:[[`0.45 micrometre`,`Most bacteria, not all`,`Bioburden and particulate testing`],[`0.22 micrometre`,`Bacteria, including Brevundimonas diminuta`,`Sterilising grade for liquids`],[`0.1 micrometre`,`Mycoplasma (Acholeplasma laidlawii)`,`Cell culture media`],[`Ultrafilter / RO`,`Viruses and pyrogens`,`Water for injection systems`],[`HEPA`,`99.97 percent of particles 0.3 micrometre`,`Clean room and LAF air supply`]],caption:`Brevundimonas (formerly Pseudomonas) diminuta at 10^7 organisms per sq cm is the challenge organism that defines a sterilising-grade 0.22 micrometre filter.`},{kind:`heading`,text:`Gaseous and radiation sterilisation`},{kind:`bullets`,items:[`Ethylene oxide is an alkylating agent: it alkylates sulphydryl, hydroxyl, carboxyl and amino groups of proteins and nucleic acids. Typical cycle 800 to 1200 mg per litre, 45 to 63 degrees C, 30 to 70 percent relative humidity, 2 to 6 h, followed by prolonged aeration.`,`Ethylene oxide hazards: flammable and explosive above about 3 percent in air (so it is diluted with carbon dioxide or nitrogen), mutagenic and carcinogenic, irritant, and it leaves toxic residues of ethylene oxide, ethylene chlorohydrin and ethylene glycol.`,`Moisture is essential for ethylene oxide activity; dried spores are highly resistant to it.`,`Gamma radiation from a cobalt-60 source at a standard absorbed dose of 25 kGy (2.5 Mrad) is the reference for prepacked disposables.`,`Ionising radiation kills by generating free radicals that damage DNA; it needs no heat, so it is called cold sterilisation.`,`Ultraviolet at 254 nm forms thymine dimers but has almost no penetration, so it disinfects air and surfaces only.`]},{kind:`table`,headers:[`Product type`,`Method of choice`,`Why`],rows:[[`Aqueous injections, LVP`,`Autoclave 121 degrees C`,`Terminal, cheap, reliable`],[`Oils, fats, glass, powders`,`Dry heat 160 to 180 degrees C`,`Steam cannot penetrate anhydrous material`],[`Thermolabile solutions`,`0.22 micrometre filtration`,`No heat applied`],[`Plastic devices, catheters`,`Ethylene oxide`,`Low temperature, good penetration`],[`Prepacked disposables, sutures`,`Gamma radiation 25 kGy`,`Penetrates the final sealed pack`],[`Clean room air`,`HEPA filtration plus UV`,`Continuous, non-destructive`]]},{kind:`callout`,tone:`mnemonic`,title:`Order of resistance to sterilisation`,text:`Prions are more resistant than bacterial spores, which beat Mycobacteria, which beat non-lipid viruses, which beat fungi, which beat vegetative bacteria; lipid-enveloped viruses are the easiest to kill.`}]},{topicId:`pm-sterilisation`,level:`advanced`,blocks:[{kind:`para`,text:`Advanced questions test survivor-curve arithmetic, the difference between overkill and bioburden-based cycle design, and the physical controls that prove a cycle actually happened.`},{kind:`formula`,expr:`Inactivation factor IF = 10 raised to (t / D)`,caption:`IF is the factor by which the population is reduced. Nt = N0 / IF. A 15 min cycle with D121 = 1 min gives IF = 10^15.`},{kind:`formula`,expr:`log D1 - log D2 = (T2 - T1) / Z`,caption:`The Z-value relationship. D1 and D2 are D values at temperatures T1 and T2. Example: D110 = 20 min and D120 = 2 min gives Z = 10 degrees C.`},{kind:`callout`,tone:`exam`,title:`Worked numerical 3`,text:`D121 of Geobacillus stearothermophilus spores is 2 min and the biological indicator carries 10^6 spores. To achieve a probability of a surviving organism of 10^-6 you need 12 log reductions, so the cycle must deliver 12 x 2 = 24 minutes at 121 degrees C, i.e. an F0 of 24.`},{kind:`heading`,text:`Cycle design philosophies`},{kind:`table`,headers:[`Approach`,`Basis`,`Typical requirement`],rows:[[`Overkill`,`Ignore bioburden, assume worst case`,`F0 of at least 12; 12 log kill of a resistant BI`],[`Bioburden-based`,`Measure and control incoming bioburden`,`Lower F0, but routine bioburden testing`],[`Bioburden plus BI`,`Combines both`,`Used for moderately labile products`],[`Aseptic processing`,`No terminal step at all`,`Media fill with contamination rate below 0.1 percent`]],caption:`A sterility assurance level of 10^-6, meaning not more than one non-sterile unit in a million, is the accepted target for terminal sterilisation.`},{kind:`heading`,text:`Proving the cycle happened`},{kind:`bullets`,items:[`Physical: calibrated thermocouples for heat distribution (empty chamber) and heat penetration (loaded, in the coldest container), pressure and time recorders.`,`Chemical indicators: autoclave tape and Brownes tubes change colour but only show that heat was applied, not that the load is sterile.`,`Bowie-Dick test: a daily porous-load test that detects residual air and steam penetration failure in a pre-vacuum autoclave.`,`Biological indicators: spore preparations of defined population and D value, the only direct measure of lethality.`,`Parametric release replaces the sterility test with fully validated physical data, and is permitted only for terminally sterilised products.`]},{kind:`table`,headers:[`Sterilisation method`,`Biological indicator`],rows:[[`Moist heat (autoclave)`,`Geobacillus stearothermophilus spores`],[`Dry heat and ethylene oxide`,`Bacillus atrophaeus spores`],[`Ionising radiation`,`Bacillus pumilus spores`],[`Low temperature steam-formaldehyde`,`Geobacillus stearothermophilus spores`],[`Hydrogen peroxide vapour`,`Geobacillus stearothermophilus spores`]],caption:`Bacillus atrophaeus was formerly named Bacillus subtilis var. niger; older books still use that name.`},{kind:`formula`,expr:`Bubble point P = 4 gamma cos(theta) / d`,caption:`P = bubble point pressure, gamma = surface tension of the wetting liquid, theta = contact angle, d = pore diameter. Smaller pores give a higher bubble point, so a low reading means an oversized pore or a damaged filter.`},{kind:`callout`,tone:`trap`,title:`What filtration does not remove`,text:`A 0.22 micrometre sterilising filter removes bacteria and fungi but not viruses, not mycoplasma reliably, and not pyrogens, because endotoxin molecules are far smaller than the pore. Depyrogenation needs dry heat at 250 degrees C, ultrafiltration, or adsorption on activated charcoal.`},{kind:`callout`,tone:`exam`,title:`Fine distinctions that get asked`,text:`Steam must be saturated and dry: superheated steam behaves like dry heat and is much less lethal, while wet steam wets the load. Formaldehyde is used as low temperature steam-formaldehyde at about 73 degrees C. Beta-propiolactone is highly virucidal but carcinogenic. Peracetic acid leaves no toxic residue, breaking down to acetic acid, water and oxygen.`}]},{topicId:`pm-sterility-validation`,level:`basic`,blocks:[{kind:`para`,text:`A sterility test asks one question: can any organism be recovered from this batch? It is a destructive test on a small sample, so it can never prove the whole batch is sterile. That is why validation of the sterilisation process and control of the manufacturing environment matter more than the test itself.`},{kind:`bullets`,items:[`Sterility test: incubate samples of the product in nutrient media and look for turbidity.`,`Two official methods: membrane filtration and direct inoculation (direct transfer).`,`Two official media: fluid thioglycollate medium and soybean casein digest medium.`,`Incubation period: 14 days, with observation at intervals and at the end.`,`Result: growth in any container means the batch fails, unless the growth is proved to come from a testing error.`]},{kind:`heading`,text:`The two media`},{kind:`table`,headers:[`Medium`,`Incubation`,`Detects`],rows:[[`Fluid thioglycollate (FTM)`,`30 to 35 degrees C`,`Anaerobic bacteria mainly, plus aerobes`],[`Soybean casein digest (SCDM)`,`20 to 25 degrees C`,`Fungi, moulds, yeasts and aerobic bacteria`]],caption:`SCDM is also called tryptone soya broth. The lower temperature suits fungi; the reducing agent in FTM suits anaerobes.`},{kind:`callout`,tone:`mnemonic`,title:`Which medium at which temperature`,text:`Thioglycollate is Thirty-five (30 to 35 degrees C, anaerobes); Soybean is the cooler one at 20 to 25 degrees C for fungi.`},{kind:`para`,text:`Fluid thioglycollate medium contains sodium thioglycollate and L-cystine as reducing agents to lower the oxygen level, a little agar to stop convection currents carrying oxygen down, and resazurin as a redox indicator. Resazurin turns pink where oxygen has entered; if the pink layer is more than the upper one third of the medium, the medium must be restored by heating once or discarded.`},{kind:`heading`,text:`Controls`},{kind:`bullets`,items:[`Negative control: medium alone, incubated to show it was sterile to begin with.`,`Positive control: medium plus a small inoculum of a known organism, to show the medium supports growth.`,`Method suitability (bacteriostasis and fungistasis) test: prove the product itself does not inhibit the test organisms.`]},{kind:`para`,text:`A clean room is a room in which the number of airborne particles and organisms is controlled. Air is supplied through HEPA filters, the room is kept at a positive pressure to the corridor outside so that air always flows outward, and the number of people inside is minimised because people are the largest single source of contamination.`}]},{topicId:`pm-sterility-validation`,level:`intermediate`,blocks:[{kind:`para`,text:`Exam questions here cluster on the choice between membrane filtration and direct inoculation, the numbers used in environmental monitoring, and clean room grades.`},{kind:`heading`,text:`Membrane filtration versus direct inoculation`},{kind:`table`,headers:[`Point`,`Membrane filtration`,`Direct inoculation`],rows:[[`Principle`,`Filter product, transfer membrane to medium`,`Add product straight into medium`],[`Membrane`,`0.45 micrometre, about 47 mm diameter`,`Not used`],[`Suits`,`Filterable liquids, oils, large volumes`,`Non-filterable solids, ointments, small volumes`],[`Antimicrobial product`,`Washed off the membrane, so no interference`,`Must be diluted or neutralised`],[`Product to medium ratio`,`Not applicable`,`Product not more than 10 percent of the medium`],[`Sensitivity`,`Higher, whole volume is sampled`,`Lower, only a small aliquot`]],caption:`Membrane filtration is the method of choice and is preferred by the pharmacopoeias whenever the product can be filtered.`},{kind:`callout`,tone:`trap`,title:`Two different pore sizes`,text:`Sterilising filtration uses 0.22 micrometre, but the sterility test membrane is 0.45 micrometre. The test membrane only needs to retain any contaminant present, and the larger pore filters viscous products faster.`},{kind:`numbered`,items:[`Aseptically filter the product through a pre-sterilised 0.45 micrometre membrane.`,`Rinse the membrane, typically three washes of 100 mL of sterile diluting fluid, to remove residual antimicrobial.`,`Cut the membrane in half, or use two canisters.`,`Transfer one half to fluid thioglycollate medium and the other to soybean casein digest medium.`,`Incubate 14 days at 30 to 35 and 20 to 25 degrees C respectively, and examine for turbidity.`]},{kind:`heading`,text:`Biological indicators for validation`},{kind:`table`,headers:[`Process`,`Indicator organism`,`Reference D value`],rows:[[`Moist heat`,`Geobacillus stearothermophilus`,`D121 about 1.5 to 3 min`],[`Dry heat`,`Bacillus atrophaeus`,`D160 about 1 to 3 min`],[`Ethylene oxide`,`Bacillus atrophaeus`,`D54 about 2.5 to 5 min`],[`Gamma radiation`,`Bacillus pumilus`,`D about 1.5 to 3 kGy`],[`Filtration`,`Brevundimonas diminuta`,`Challenge 10^7 per sq cm`]],caption:`A biological indicator carries a defined spore population, usually 10^6, of known resistance.`},{kind:`heading`,text:`Validation stages`},{kind:`bullets`,items:[`Design qualification: the specification is fit for purpose before purchase.`,`Installation qualification: the equipment is installed as specified, utilities correct.`,`Operational qualification: it performs across the whole operating range, empty chamber heat distribution done here.`,`Performance qualification: it performs with the actual load, using heat penetration probes and biological indicators.`,`Revalidation: after any change and at a defined interval, usually yearly for autoclaves.`]},{kind:`heading`,text:`Environmental monitoring`},{kind:`table`,headers:[`Technique`,`What it samples`,`Unit`],rows:[[`Settle plate, 90 mm, 4 h exposure`,`Organisms settling from air`,`cfu per plate`],[`Active air sampler (slit-to-agar, sieve)`,`A measured volume of air`,`cfu per cubic metre`],[`Contact (RODAC) plate, 55 mm`,`Flat surfaces`,`cfu per plate`],[`Swab`,`Irregular surfaces and equipment`,`cfu per swab`],[`Glove print, five fingers`,`Operator hands`,`cfu per glove`],[`Particle counter`,`Non-viable particles`,`Particles per cubic metre`]]},{kind:`heading`,text:`Clean room grades`},{kind:`table`,headers:[`EU GMP grade`,`ISO 14644 at rest`,`Old federal class`,`Microbial limit, air`],rows:[[`A (LAF work zone)`,`ISO 5`,`Class 100`,`Less than 1 cfu per cubic metre`],[`B (background to A)`,`ISO 5`,`Class 100`,`10 cfu per cubic metre`],[`C`,`ISO 7`,`Class 10 000`,`100 cfu per cubic metre`],[`D`,`ISO 8`,`Class 100 000`,`200 cfu per cubic metre`]],caption:`Grade A is used for aseptic filling and open ampoule handling. ISO 5 allows 3520 particles of 0.5 micrometre or larger per cubic metre.`},{kind:`callout`,tone:`exam`,title:`Numbers to carry into the hall`,text:`Grade A unidirectional air velocity 0.36 to 0.54 metre per second (usually quoted as 0.45). Air changes of not less than 20 per hour in Grades B, C and D. Pressure differential between adjacent grades of 10 to 15 pascals. Media fill acceptance: with 5000 units or more filled, not more than one contaminated unit, i.e. a contamination rate below 0.1 percent.`}]},{topicId:`pm-sterility-validation`,level:`advanced`,blocks:[{kind:`para`,text:`The advanced layer is the statistics of the sterility test, pyrogen and endotoxin testing, and the rules for interpreting a failure.`},{kind:`callout`,tone:`trap`,title:`Why the sterility test is weak`,text:`If only 0.1 percent of a batch is contaminated and 20 units are tested, the probability of detecting the contamination is only about 2 percent. The test can therefore condemn a bad batch but can never certify a good one; assurance comes from validation and parametric release.`},{kind:`bullets`,items:[`Sample size rises with batch size: for parenterals up to 100 containers, 10 percent or 4 containers whichever is greater; 101 to 500 containers, 10 containers; more than 500, 2 percent or 20 containers whichever is less.`,`The whole contents are tested when the container holds less than 1 mL; larger containers are sampled to a defined minimum volume.`,`A first-stage failure may be invalidated only if there is documented evidence of a fault in the aseptic technique or the facility, in which case the test is repeated with the same number of units.`]},{kind:`heading`,text:`Pyrogens and endotoxin`},{kind:`para`,text:`Pyrogens are fever-producing substances; the most important are the lipopolysaccharides of Gram negative cell walls, whose active moiety is lipid A. They are heat stable and pass through a sterilising filter, so a sterile solution can still be pyrogenic.`},{kind:`table`,headers:[`Test`,`Basis`,`Note`],rows:[[`Rabbit pyrogen test`,`Rise in rectal temperature after IV dose`,`Detects all pyrogens, in vivo`],[`LAL gel clot`,`Clotting of Limulus amoebocyte lysate`,`Limit test, simplest, most used`],[`LAL turbidimetric`,`Turbidity development rate`,`Quantitative`],[`LAL chromogenic`,`Release of p-nitroaniline, read at 405 nm`,`Quantitative, most sensitive`],[`Monocyte activation test`,`Cytokine release from human cells`,`Replaces rabbits, detects non-endotoxin pyrogens`]]},{kind:`formula`,expr:`Endotoxin limit = K / M`,caption:`K = threshold pyrogenic dose, 5.0 EU per kg per hour for parenteral routes and 0.2 EU per kg for intrathecal; M = maximum human dose per kg per hour. Example: a drug with M = 1 mg per kg gives a limit of 5 EU per mg.`},{kind:`callout`,tone:`exam`,title:`Removing pyrogens`,text:`Endotoxin is destroyed by dry heat at 250 degrees C for 30 minutes (glass and metal), by strong alkali or oxidising agents, and removed by ultrafiltration, reverse osmosis or adsorption onto activated charcoal. Autoclaving and 0.22 micrometre filtration do not remove it.`},{kind:`bullets`,items:[`Media fill (process simulation): sterile growth medium is run through the entire aseptic filling line in place of the product, then incubated and read for turbidity.`,`Isolators and restricted access barrier systems separate the operator from the critical zone, and give lower contamination rates than a conventional Grade A within Grade B suite.`,`Alert level: an early warning that a trend is drifting. Action level: the limit that triggers investigation and corrective action. Both are set from historical data, and the alert level always sits below the action level.`,`Water systems are monitored for total viable count, endotoxin and conductivity; purified water is typically limited to 100 cfu per mL and water for injection to 10 cfu per 100 mL with endotoxin below 0.25 EU per mL.`]}]},{topicId:`pm-microbial-assay`,level:`basic`,blocks:[{kind:`para`,text:`A microbial assay measures how much of a substance is present by measuring its effect on a living organism. For an antibiotic you measure how much it stops growth; for a vitamin you measure how much it supports growth. It gives biological potency, which a chemical assay cannot.`},{kind:`bullets`,items:[`Potency is expressed in international units or in micrograms of a reference standard.`,`The sample is always compared with an official reference standard run in the same experiment.`,`The response is compared over a range of doses, not at a single dose.`,`Antibiotic assay uses an organism sensitive to the antibiotic; vitamin assay uses an organism that cannot make the vitamin.`]},{kind:`heading`,text:`The two antibiotic methods`},{kind:`table`,headers:[`Method`,`What is measured`,`Also called`],rows:[[`Cylinder-plate`,`Diameter of the zone of inhibition on agar`,`Cup-plate, agar diffusion`],[`Turbidimetric`,`Reduction in turbidity of a broth culture`,`Tube assay`]]},{kind:`para`,text:`In the cylinder-plate method a Petri dish is poured with a base layer of agar and then a seed layer inoculated with the test organism. Small stainless steel cylinders are placed on the surface and filled with standard and sample solutions. The antibiotic diffuses outward, and where its concentration is still high enough to inhibit the organism no growth appears, leaving a clear circular zone whose diameter is measured.`},{kind:`callout`,tone:`exam`,title:`The relationship to remember`,text:`In agar diffusion the zone diameter is proportional to the logarithm of the concentration, not to the concentration itself. Plotting zone diameter against log dose gives a straight line.`},{kind:`para`,text:`In the turbidimetric method the antibiotic is added to a liquid culture and the tubes are incubated for only three to four hours at 37 degrees C. Growth is measured as absorbance, usually near 530 nm. The more antibiotic, the less growth, so absorbance falls as dose rises.`},{kind:`callout`,tone:`trap`,title:`When turbidimetry cannot be used`,text:`The turbidimetric method reads light scattering, so a coloured, turbid or particulate sample gives a false reading. Such samples must go to the cylinder-plate method.`}]},{topicId:`pm-microbial-assay`,level:`intermediate`,blocks:[{kind:`para`,text:`You need the design of both assays, the test organisms paired to each drug or vitamin, and the definitions and determination of MIC and MBC.`},{kind:`table`,headers:[`Point`,`Cylinder-plate`,`Turbidimetric`],rows:[[`Response measured`,`Zone diameter in mm`,`Absorbance, about 530 nm`],[`Dose-response`,`Linear against log dose`,`Linear against log dose over a narrow range`],[`Incubation`,`16 to 18 h at 32 to 37 degrees C`,`3 to 4 h at 37 degrees C`],[`Time taken`,`Overnight`,`Same working day`],[`Precision`,`Good`,`Better, but narrower range`],[`Coloured or turbid samples`,`Acceptable`,`Not acceptable`],[`Typical designs`,`2 x 2, 3 x 3, 5 x 1 standard curve`,`1 + 1 or standard curve`]]},{kind:`bullets`,items:[`Cylinders are stainless steel or porcelain, about 8 mm outside diameter, 6 mm inside diameter and 10 mm high, placed six to a plate on a 2.8 cm radius circle.`,`Standard and sample alternate around the plate so that plate-to-plate and positional variation cancel out.`,`A one-level (2 x 2) assay uses one dose of standard and one of sample on the same plate; a 3 x 3 assay uses three dose levels of each and allows a full statistical validity check.`,`The 5 x 1 design builds a standard curve from five dose levels of the standard on separate plates and reads the sample against it.`]},{kind:`heading`,text:`Test organisms`},{kind:`table`,headers:[`Substance assayed`,`Usual test organism`],rows:[[`Penicillin`,`Staphylococcus aureus`],[`Streptomycin`,`Bacillus subtilis`],[`Tetracycline`,`Bacillus cereus or Staphylococcus aureus`],[`Neomycin`,`Staphylococcus epidermidis`],[`Amphotericin B and nystatin`,`Saccharomyces cerevisiae`],[`Cyanocobalamin (B12)`,`Lactobacillus leichmannii`],[`Riboflavin (B2)`,`Lactobacillus casei`],[`Nicotinic acid and pantothenic acid`,`Lactobacillus plantarum`]],caption:`Note the pattern: antibacterial assays use a sensitive bacterium or yeast; water-soluble vitamin assays use a lactobacillus that cannot synthesise the vitamin.`},{kind:`heading`,text:`Microbial assay of vitamins`},{kind:`numbered`,items:[`Prepare a basal medium complete in every nutrient except the vitamin being assayed.`,`Add graded amounts of the reference vitamin to one set of tubes and graded amounts of the sample to another.`,`Inoculate with the vitamin-requiring organism and incubate, usually 16 to 24 h.`,`Measure growth turbidimetrically, or titrate the lactic acid produced (acidimetric method).`,`Read the sample potency from the standard curve of growth against log dose.`]},{kind:`callout`,tone:`exam`,title:`Direction of the response`,text:`In an antibiotic assay growth falls as dose rises; in a vitamin assay growth rises as dose rises. The vitamin assay is therefore a growth-promotion assay, the antibiotic assay a growth-inhibition assay.`},{kind:`heading`,text:`MIC and MBC`},{kind:`table`,headers:[`Term`,`Definition`,`How determined`],rows:[[`MIC`,`Lowest concentration preventing visible growth in 18 to 24 h`,`Broth or agar dilution series`],[`MBC`,`Lowest concentration killing 99.9 percent of the inoculum`,`Subculture clear tubes onto drug-free agar`],[`MBEC`,`Lowest concentration eradicating a biofilm`,`Biofilm device assay`]],caption:`MBC is always equal to or greater than MIC. An MBC to MIC ratio of 4 or less means the drug is bactericidal; a ratio above 4 indicates tolerance.`},{kind:`bullets`,items:[`Broth macrodilution uses tubes, microdilution uses a 96-well plate and is the routine method.`,`Agar dilution incorporates the drug into the agar and spots multiple isolates on each plate.`,`The E-test uses a plastic strip carrying a continuous antibiotic gradient; the MIC is read where the elliptical zone edge crosses the strip.`,`Disc diffusion (Kirby-Bauer) gives a zone diameter interpreted as sensitive, intermediate or resistant, not a numerical MIC.`]}]},{topicId:`pm-microbial-assay`,level:`advanced`,blocks:[{kind:`para`,text:`Advanced questions ask about the physics of the diffusion zone, the statistical model behind the assay, and the sources of error.`},{kind:`formula`,expr:`x squared = 4 D t (ln C - ln C0)`,caption:`The Cooper-Woodman diffusion equation. x = zone radius, D = diffusion coefficient, t = time before growth becomes visible, C = applied concentration, C0 = critical inhibitory concentration. It shows why the square of the zone radius, not the diameter itself, is strictly proportional to log concentration.`},{kind:`bullets`,items:[`Larger zones are given by a thinner seed layer, a smaller inoculum, a longer pre-diffusion period at low temperature, and a drug of high diffusivity.`,`Pre-incubation diffusion at 4 degrees C for 1 to 2 h lets the drug spread before growth starts and sharpens the zone edge.`,`A heavy inoculum shrinks the zone and can make a potent sample read as sub-potent.`,`Aminoglycoside zones widen at alkaline pH; tetracycline is more active at acid pH, so buffer control is critical.`]},{kind:`heading`,text:`Statistical design`},{kind:`bullets`,items:[`The standard model is the parallel line assay on log dose: standard and sample lines must be straight and parallel for the assay to be valid.`,`Validity is tested by analysis of variance, checking regression, deviation from parallelism and deviation from linearity.`,`Potency is calculated from the horizontal distance between the two parallel lines, and reported with fiducial (confidence) limits, usually required to lie within 95 to 105 percent of the estimate.`,`Randomisation of cylinder positions and use of a Latin square design remove positional bias on the plate.`]},{kind:`table`,headers:[`Assay type`,`Advantage`,`Limitation`],rows:[[`Microbial (biological)`,`Measures true biological activity`,`Precision only about 5 to 10 percent`],[`HPLC (chemical)`,`Precise, fast, specific`,`Measures molecule, not activity`],[`Immunoassay`,`Very sensitive, small samples`,`Cross-reacts with inactive metabolites`]],caption:`Microbial assay is still mandatory for antibiotics such as polymyxin and bacitracin that are mixtures of closely related actives with different potencies.`},{kind:`callout`,tone:`trap`,title:`Why a chemical assay is not enough`,text:`A chemical assay counts molecules whether or not they are active. If an antibiotic has partly degraded into an inactive but chemically similar product, HPLC may still report full content while the microbial assay correctly reports reduced potency. That is why potency of antibiotics is defined biologically, in units.`},{kind:`callout`,tone:`exam`,title:`Units worth knowing`,text:`One international unit of penicillin G equals 0.6 microgram of the sodium salt, so 1 mg equals about 1667 IU. Potencies of streptomycin and tetracycline are quoted in micrograms of base per milligram. Always state whether the potency refers to the base or to a salt.`}]},{topicId:`pm-immunology-vaccines`,level:`basic`,blocks:[{kind:`para`,text:`Immunity is the ability of the body to resist infection. It comes in two layers: an innate layer you are born with that acts at once on anything foreign, and an acquired layer that learns each specific organism and remembers it.`},{kind:`table`,headers:[`Feature`,`Innate immunity`,`Acquired immunity`],rows:[[`Present from`,`Birth`,`After exposure`],[`Specificity`,`None, acts on any invader`,`Highly specific to one antigen`],[`Memory`,`Absent`,`Present, basis of vaccination`],[`Speed`,`Minutes to hours`,`Days on first exposure`],[`Main players`,`Skin, mucus, lysozyme, neutrophils, macrophages, NK cells, complement, interferon`,`B lymphocytes, T lymphocytes, antibodies`]]},{kind:`heading`,text:`Ways acquired immunity is obtained`},{kind:`table`,headers:[`Type`,`Natural`,`Artificial`],rows:[[`Active (body makes antibody)`,`Recovery from infection`,`Vaccination`],[`Passive (antibody given ready-made)`,`Maternal IgG across placenta, IgA in breast milk`,`Antiserum, tetanus immunoglobulin, antivenom`]],caption:`Active immunity is slow to appear but long lasting with memory. Passive immunity is immediate but short lived and leaves no memory.`},{kind:`bullets`,items:[`Antigen: a substance the immune system recognises as foreign and responds to. Usually a protein or polysaccharide above about 10 kDa.`,`Epitope (antigenic determinant): the small part of the antigen the antibody actually binds.`,`Hapten: a small molecule that cannot provoke a response on its own but does so once linked to a carrier protein. Penicillin is the classic drug hapten.`,`Antibody (immunoglobulin): the protein made by plasma cells that binds the antigen specifically.`,`Antigenicity is the ability to be bound; immunogenicity is the ability to provoke a response. A hapten has the first without the second.`]},{kind:`heading`,text:`Shape of an antibody`},{kind:`para`,text:`An antibody is a Y-shaped glycoprotein made of two identical heavy chains and two identical light chains held by disulphide bridges. The two arms of the Y carry the antigen-binding sites and are called the Fab fragments; the stem is the Fc fragment, which binds complement and cell receptors and decides what happens to the antigen once it is caught.`},{kind:`callout`,tone:`mnemonic`,title:`Fab and Fc`,text:`Fab = fragment antigen binding (two per IgG). Fc = fragment crystallisable, the effector end. Papain gives two Fab plus one Fc; pepsin cuts lower down and gives one F(ab)2 that is still bivalent.`},{kind:`para`,text:`A vaccine works by giving the body a harmless form of an organism or its toxin so that memory cells are made. On real exposure the secondary response is faster, larger and mostly IgG, and the infection is stopped before it causes disease.`}]},{topicId:`pm-immunology-vaccines`,level:`intermediate`,blocks:[{kind:`para`,text:`The immunoglobulin class table, the four hypersensitivity types with their named examples, and the vaccine classification with examples are asked almost every year.`},{kind:`heading`,text:`Immunoglobulin classes`},{kind:`table`,headers:[`Class`,`Share of serum Ig`,`Form`,`Key property`],rows:[[`IgG`,`75 to 80 percent`,`Monomer`,`Only Ig crossing the placenta; secondary response; opsonisation`],[`IgA`,`10 to 15 percent`,`Dimer in secretions`,`Mucosal defence; found in colostrum, saliva, tears`],[`IgM`,`5 to 10 percent`,`Pentamer`,`First antibody of the primary response; best complement fixer`],[`IgD`,`Less than 1 percent`,`Monomer`,`Antigen receptor on the naive B cell surface`],[`IgE`,`Less than 0.01 percent`,`Monomer`,`Binds mast cells; type I allergy and worm infection`]],caption:`IgM is the largest (about 900 kDa, hence millionaire) and IgG has the longest half-life, about 23 days.`},{kind:`callout`,tone:`trap`,title:`IgM versus IgG in diagnosis`,text:`A rise in specific IgM means recent or current infection because IgM appears first and disappears in weeks. IgG persists for years and only indicates past exposure or vaccination. IgM cannot cross the placenta, so IgM in cord blood proves intrauterine infection.`},{kind:`heading`,text:`Humoral versus cell-mediated immunity`},{kind:`table`,headers:[`Point`,`Humoral`,`Cell mediated`],rows:[[`Cell type`,`B lymphocyte to plasma cell`,`T lymphocyte, CD4 and CD8`],[`Mediator`,`Antibody`,`Cytokines and cytotoxic T cells`],[`Best against`,`Extracellular bacteria and toxins`,`Intracellular organisms, fungi, tumours`],[`Transferred by`,`Serum`,`Lymphocytes, not serum`],[`Example test`,`Antibody titre, ELISA`,`Mantoux (tuberculin) skin test`],[`Role in grafts`,`Hyperacute rejection`,`Acute rejection and graft versus host`]]},{kind:`heading`,text:`Hypersensitivity, Coombs and Gell types`},{kind:`table`,headers:[`Type`,`Mediator`,`Timing`,`Examples`],rows:[[`I Anaphylactic`,`IgE on mast cells, histamine`,`Minutes`,`Anaphylaxis, atopic asthma, hay fever, urticaria`],[`II Cytotoxic`,`IgG or IgM against cell surface antigen, complement`,`Hours`,`Mismatched transfusion, Rh haemolytic disease, Goodpasture`],[`III Immune complex`,`Antigen-antibody complexes, complement, neutrophils`,`6 to 12 h`,`Arthus reaction, serum sickness, SLE, post-streptococcal nephritis`],[`IV Delayed`,`T cells and macrophages, no antibody`,`24 to 72 h`,`Tuberculin test, contact dermatitis, graft rejection`]]},{kind:`callout`,tone:`mnemonic`,title:`ACID`,text:`Type I Anaphylactic, II Cytotoxic, III Immune complex, IV Delayed. Types I to III are antibody mediated and can be transferred by serum; only type IV is cell mediated and needs lymphocytes.`},{kind:`heading`,text:`Vaccine types`},{kind:`table`,headers:[`Type`,`Examples`,`Note`],rows:[[`Live attenuated`,`BCG, oral polio (Sabin), MMR, varicella, yellow fever, rotavirus`,`Strong lasting immunity; avoid in pregnancy and immunodeficiency`],[`Killed / inactivated`,`Injectable polio (Salk), rabies, whole-cell pertussis, hepatitis A, cholera`,`Safe but needs boosters and an adjuvant`],[`Toxoid`,`Diphtheria, tetanus`,`Exotoxin detoxified with formaldehyde`],[`Subunit / recombinant`,`Hepatitis B surface antigen, acellular pertussis, HPV virus-like particle`,`Purified antigen only, fewest side effects`],[`Polysaccharide`,`23-valent pneumococcal, meningococcal`,`T-independent, poor in children under 2, no memory`],[`Conjugate`,`Hib, pneumococcal conjugate, meningococcal C`,`Polysaccharide linked to protein, becomes T-dependent with memory`],[`Nucleic acid / vector`,`mRNA and adenovirus-vector COVID-19 vaccines`,`Host cell makes the antigen`]]},{kind:`bullets`,items:[`An adjuvant is a substance that increases the immune response to an antigen without being antigenic itself.`,`Aluminium hydroxide and aluminium phosphate (alum) are the adjuvants most used in human vaccines; they form a depot and bias the response toward antibody production.`,`Freund complete adjuvant is mineral oil plus killed mycobacteria; it is far too irritant for humans and is used only in animals. Freund incomplete adjuvant omits the mycobacteria.`,`Newer adjuvants: MF59 squalene emulsion, monophosphoryl lipid A, saponin QS-21, CpG oligonucleotides.`]},{kind:`callout`,tone:`exam`,title:`Live versus killed in one line`,text:`Live attenuated vaccines give both humoral and cell-mediated immunity, often after a single dose, but need a cold chain and can revert. Killed vaccines give mainly humoral immunity, need multiple doses and an adjuvant, but are stable and safe in the immunocompromised.`}]},{topicId:`pm-immunology-vaccines`,level:`advanced`,blocks:[{kind:`para`,text:`At the top level you need complement pathways, MHC restriction, the quantitative difference between the primary and secondary responses, and the immunology behind drug allergy.`},{kind:`table`,headers:[`Feature`,`Primary response`,`Secondary response`],rows:[[`Lag period`,`5 to 10 days`,`1 to 3 days`],[`Peak titre`,`Low`,`10 to 100 times higher`],[`Predominant class`,`IgM`,`IgG`],[`Affinity`,`Low`,`High, by affinity maturation`],[`Duration`,`Short`,`Long`],[`Responding cell`,`Naive B cell`,`Memory B cell`]],caption:`This difference is the entire rationale for a booster dose.`},{kind:`heading`,text:`Complement`},{kind:`bullets`,items:[`Classical pathway is triggered by antigen-antibody complexes acting on C1q; IgM is far more efficient at this than IgG because one pentamer presents two adjacent Fc regions.`,`Alternative pathway is triggered directly by microbial surfaces without antibody; the lectin pathway is triggered by mannose-binding lectin.`,`All three converge on C3 convertase, then on C5 to C9 forming the membrane attack complex that lyses the cell.`,`C3a and C5a are anaphylatoxins that cause mast cell degranulation; C5a is also the strongest chemotactic factor; C3b is the main opsonin.`]},{kind:`table`,headers:[`MHC class`,`Found on`,`Presents to`,`Peptide source`],rows:[[`Class I (HLA-A, B, C)`,`All nucleated cells`,`CD8 cytotoxic T cells`,`Endogenous, e.g. viral protein`],[`Class II (HLA-DP, DQ, DR)`,`Macrophages, B cells, dendritic cells`,`CD4 helper T cells`,`Exogenous, phagocytosed`]]},{kind:`callout`,tone:`exam`,title:`Drug allergy mechanisms`,text:`Penicillin is a hapten: its beta-lactam ring opens and the penicilloyl group binds lysine residues of serum proteins, and this conjugate provokes IgE. Penicillin can also cause type II haemolytic anaemia and type III serum sickness, so one drug can produce three different hypersensitivity types.`},{kind:`bullets`,items:[`Monoclonal antibodies are made by fusing an antibody-producing B cell with a myeloma cell to give a hybridoma, then selecting in HAT medium. Naming: -omab mouse, -ximab chimeric, -zumab humanised, -umab fully human.`,`An antitoxin such as tetanus antitoxin gives immediate passive protection; horse-derived antiserum risks serum sickness, so human tetanus immunoglobulin is preferred.`,`Herd immunity means enough of the population is immune that transmission cannot be sustained; the threshold rises with the basic reproduction number.`,`Cold chain: most vaccines are stored at 2 to 8 degrees C. Oral polio and measles vaccines are the most heat sensitive; adsorbed toxoids such as DPT are damaged irreversibly by freezing, so the shake test is used to detect a frozen vial.`]},{kind:`callout`,tone:`trap`,title:`Which vaccines must never be frozen`,text:`Aluminium-adsorbed vaccines (DPT, DT, TT, hepatitis B) lose potency permanently on freezing because the adsorbed antigen is released from the adjuvant. Freezing does not harm live attenuated oral polio vaccine.`}]},{topicId:`pm-fermentation`,level:`basic`,blocks:[{kind:`para`,text:`Fermentation technology is the use of microorganisms on an industrial scale to make useful products: antibiotics, vitamins, organic acids, enzymes, amino acids and recombinant proteins. The vessel in which it happens is called a fermenter or bioreactor.`},{kind:`heading`,text:`Types of fermentation`},{kind:`bullets`,items:[`Batch: everything is added at the start, nothing is added or removed until harvest. Simple, low contamination risk, but productivity is low.`,`Fed-batch: nutrients are fed in during the run without removing broth. Keeps the substrate low, avoids catabolite repression, and is the standard for penicillin.`,`Continuous: fresh medium enters and broth leaves at the same rate, holding the culture in a steady state. Highest productivity, but the greatest contamination and mutation risk.`,`Submerged: cells grow throughout a liquid medium; almost all modern antibiotic production.`,`Surface or solid state: cells grow on the surface of a liquid or a moist solid, as in traditional citric acid trays or koji.`]},{kind:`heading`,text:`Parts of a stirred tank fermenter`},{kind:`bullets`,items:[`Vessel: cylindrical stainless steel 316L, height to diameter about 2:1 to 3:1, filled to 70 to 80 percent working volume.`,`Agitator with impellers: usually Rushton six-blade disc turbines, to mix and break air bubbles.`,`Baffles: four vertical strips, about one tenth of the vessel diameter, to stop a vortex forming.`,`Sparger: distributes sterile air at the bottom, below the lowest impeller.`,`Jacket or internal coils: heating for sterilisation and cooling to remove metabolic heat.`,`Probes: temperature, pH, dissolved oxygen, and level.`,`Antifoam system: a foam sensor plus antifoam addition or a mechanical foam breaker.`,`Aseptic seals, sampling port, inoculation port and feed ports.`]},{kind:`callout`,tone:`exam`,title:`Why baffles matter`,text:`Without baffles the whole liquid rotates with the impeller as a solid body, a vortex forms, air is drawn in unevenly and mixing collapses. Baffles convert rotational flow into top-to-bottom turbulence, which is what actually transfers oxygen.`},{kind:`para`,text:`Air fed to a fermenter must itself be sterile. It is compressed, cooled and passed through depth or membrane filters, typically hydrophobic PTFE cartridges of 0.22 micrometre rating. Aeration rate is quoted in vvm, volumes of air per volume of medium per minute, and is usually 0.5 to 1.0 vvm.`},{kind:`callout`,tone:`mnemonic`,title:`Primary versus secondary metabolite`,text:`Primary metabolites appear in the growth phase (trophophase) and the cell needs them to live: ethanol, lactic acid, amino acids, vitamins. Secondary metabolites appear after growth slows (idiophase) and the cell can live without them: antibiotics, alkaloids, pigments, toxins.`},{kind:`para`,text:`Once fermentation ends, the product has to be recovered from a dilute, dirty broth. That whole recovery and purification sequence is called downstream processing, and it often costs more than the fermentation itself.`}]},{topicId:`pm-fermentation`,level:`intermediate`,blocks:[{kind:`para`,text:`For the exam, fix the organism, medium, precursor and recovery route for the four classic products, and be able to order the steps of downstream processing.`},{kind:`table`,headers:[`Point`,`Primary metabolite`,`Secondary metabolite`],rows:[[`Phase produced`,`Trophophase (log)`,`Idiophase (stationary)`],[`Needed for growth`,`Yes`,`No`],[`Distribution`,`Common to most organisms`,`Restricted to a few species`],[`Relation to growth`,`Growth associated`,`Non growth associated`],[`Examples`,`Ethanol, lactic acid, citric acid, amino acids, enzymes`,`Penicillin, streptomycin, ergot alkaloids, gibberellin`]],caption:`Citric acid is the standard exception people forget: it is a primary metabolite even though it is made by a mould.`},{kind:`heading`,text:`Downstream processing, in order`},{kind:`numbered`,items:[`Solid-liquid separation: filtration, rotary vacuum filtration or centrifugation, sometimes after flocculation.`,`Cell disruption, only if the product is intracellular: high-pressure homogeniser, bead mill, ultrasonication or lysozyme.`,`Primary isolation and concentration: solvent extraction, adsorption, ion exchange, precipitation, ultrafiltration or two-phase aqueous extraction.`,`Purification: chromatography (ion exchange, affinity, gel filtration) or crystallisation.`,`Polishing and formulation: crystallisation, spray drying or freeze drying, then sterile filling.`]},{kind:`heading`,text:`Penicillin`},{kind:`bullets`,items:[`Organism: Penicillium chrysogenum (the original strain was P. notatum).`,`Process: fed-batch submerged aerobic culture, 6 to 7 days.`,`Medium: corn steep liquor with lactose or slowly fed glucose, plus ammonium and mineral salts.`,`Precursor: phenylacetic acid directs synthesis to penicillin G; phenoxyacetic acid gives penicillin V.`,`Conditions: 24 to 26 degrees C, pH 6.5 to 7.5, aeration about 0.5 to 1.0 vvm.`,`Recovery: filter off mycelium, acidify to pH 2 to 2.5 and extract into amyl acetate or butyl acetate, back-extract into phosphate buffer at pH 7.5, then crystallise as the potassium salt.`]},{kind:`callout`,tone:`trap`,title:`Why glucose is fed slowly`,text:`A high glucose concentration represses penicillin synthetase, so batch culture on excess glucose gives good growth and almost no penicillin. Lactose is used because it is consumed slowly, or glucose is fed continuously at a low rate: this is exactly what fed-batch operation is for.`},{kind:`heading`,text:`Streptomycin`},{kind:`bullets`,items:[`Organism: Streptomyces griseus, an actinomycete.`,`Medium: soybean meal as nitrogen source with glucose, 27 to 30 degrees C, pH around 7 to 8, 5 to 7 days, vigorous aeration.`,`Recovery: the molecule is a strong base, so it is adsorbed on activated charcoal or a weakly acidic cation exchange resin, eluted, decolourised and precipitated as the sulphate.`]},{kind:`heading`,text:`Vitamin B12`},{kind:`bullets`,items:[`Organisms: Propionibacterium shermanii or freudenreichii (an initial anaerobic phase followed by a short aerobic phase) and Pseudomonas denitrificans.`,`Cobalt chloride must be added to the medium because cobalt sits at the centre of the corrin ring, and 5,6-dimethylbenzimidazole is added as the precursor of the nucleotide base.`,`The vitamin is intracellular, so cells are harvested and heated, then the extracted corrinoids are converted to the stable cyanocobalamin form with cyanide and purified.`]},{kind:`heading`,text:`Citric acid`},{kind:`bullets`,items:[`Organism: Aspergillus niger, on beet or cane molasses, by surface tray culture or submerged culture.`,`Trace metals must be deficient, iron and manganese above all; potassium ferrocyanide is added to precipitate excess iron.`,`The pH is held low, below about 2, which suppresses the competing formation of oxalic and gluconic acid.`,`Recovery: neutralise with lime to precipitate calcium citrate, filter, then treat the precipitate with sulphuric acid to liberate citric acid and leave calcium sulphate, followed by decolourisation and crystallisation.`]},{kind:`table`,headers:[`Product`,`Organism`,`Key process point`],rows:[[`Penicillin G`,`Penicillium chrysogenum`,`Phenylacetic acid precursor, fed-batch`],[`Streptomycin`,`Streptomyces griseus`,`Ion exchange recovery`],[`Vitamin B12`,`Propionibacterium or Pseudomonas denitrificans`,`Cobalt salt in medium`],[`Citric acid`,`Aspergillus niger`,`Low iron and manganese, low pH`],[`Ethanol`,`Saccharomyces cerevisiae`,`Anaerobic, distillation recovery`],[`Griseofulvin`,`Penicillium griseofulvum`,`Antifungal, chloride in medium`]]}]},{topicId:`pm-fermentation`,level:`advanced`,blocks:[{kind:`para`,text:`The advanced layer is quantitative: growth kinetics, oxygen transfer, scale-up criteria and strain improvement.`},{kind:`formula`,expr:`mu = mu(max) x S / (Ks + S)`,caption:`The Monod equation. mu = specific growth rate per hour, mu(max) = maximum specific growth rate, S = limiting substrate concentration, Ks = substrate concentration at half of mu(max). It has the same form as the Michaelis-Menten equation.`},{kind:`formula`,expr:`D = F / V; at steady state in a chemostat D = mu`,caption:`D = dilution rate per hour, F = medium flow rate, V = culture volume. If D is raised above mu(max) the culture is washed out of the vessel.`},{kind:`formula`,expr:`OTR = kLa x (C* - CL)`,caption:`OTR = oxygen transfer rate, kLa = volumetric mass transfer coefficient per hour, C* = saturation dissolved oxygen concentration, CL = actual dissolved oxygen in the broth. Oxygen is poorly soluble in water, so kLa usually limits an aerobic fermentation.`},{kind:`bullets`,items:[`kLa is increased by faster agitation, higher aeration rate, better impeller design and higher back pressure, and is reduced by high broth viscosity and by antifoam agents.`,`Scale-up is done at constant kLa, constant power input per unit volume, constant impeller tip speed or constant mixing time. No single criterion works for every system, so constant kLa is the usual compromise.`,`Filamentous broths such as Penicillium are pseudoplastic and non-Newtonian, so viscosity falls as shear rises; this makes oxygen transfer at the vessel wall worse than in the impeller zone.`]},{kind:`heading`,text:`Improving the strain and the inoculum`},{kind:`bullets`,items:[`Strain improvement: random mutation with UV or nitrosoguanidine followed by screening, protoplast fusion between strains, and recombinant DNA technology for defined changes.`,`Auxotrophic and analogue-resistant mutants are used to break feedback inhibition, which is how lysine and glutamate are overproduced by Corynebacterium glutamicum.`,`Inoculum development (the seed train) scales the culture through slant, shake flask and seed fermenter so that the production vessel receives 5 to 10 percent volume of a young, actively growing culture.`,`Media may be sterilised in batch inside the vessel or by continuous high-temperature short-time treatment, which damages heat-labile nutrients far less.`]},{kind:`table`,headers:[`Fermenter type`,`How it mixes`,`Best for`],rows:[[`Stirred tank`,`Mechanical impeller`,`General purpose, high kLa`],[`Airlift`,`Density difference between riser and downcomer`,`Shear-sensitive cells, low energy`],[`Bubble column`,`Sparged gas only, no impeller`,`Simple, low shear, poorer mixing`],[`Packed bed`,`Flow over immobilised cells`,`Immobilised enzyme or cell processes`],[`Fluidised bed`,`Upflow suspends carrier particles`,`Immobilised cells, waste treatment`]]},{kind:`callout`,tone:`exam`,title:`Facts that decide a mark`,text:`Penicillin acylase converts penicillin G to 6-aminopenicillanic acid, the nucleus for all semisynthetic penicillins. Cephalosporin C comes from Acremonium chrysogenum (formerly Cephalosporium acremonium) and yields 7-aminocephalosporanic acid. Ergot alkaloids come from Claviceps purpurea and gibberellic acid from Gibberella fujikuroi.`},{kind:`callout`,tone:`trap`,title:`Contamination in continuous culture`,text:`Continuous fermentation gives the highest volumetric productivity but is rarely used for antibiotics. Over weeks of operation a single contaminant or a faster-growing non-producing mutant will out-compete the production strain, so batch and fed-batch remain the industrial standard.`}]}],t=[{id:`q-pm-basics-staining-1`,topicId:`pm-basics-staining`,level:`basic`,difficulty:`easy`,stem:`The ribosome of a bacterial cell is of which type?`,options:[`80S with 60S and 40S subunits`,`70S with 60S and 30S subunits`,`60S with 40S and 30S subunits`,`70S with 50S and 30S subunits`],answer:3,explanation:`Prokaryotes have 70S ribosomes made of 50S and 30S subunits; eukaryotic cytoplasmic ribosomes are 80S (60S plus 40S). The 80S choice is the eukaryotic ribosome, which is why aminoglycosides and macrolides that bind 30S or 50S are selectively toxic to bacteria. Note that Svedberg units are sedimentation coefficients and do not add arithmetically.`,tag:`Frequently asked`},{id:`q-pm-basics-staining-2`,topicId:`pm-basics-staining`,level:`basic`,difficulty:`easy`,stem:`In Gram staining, which reagent acts as the mordant?`,options:[`Crystal violet`,`Ethyl alcohol`,`Safranin`,`Grams iodine`],answer:3,explanation:`Grams iodine is the mordant: it combines with crystal violet inside the cell to form a large insoluble crystal violet-iodine complex. Crystal violet itself is the primary stain, alcohol the decolouriser and safranin the counterstain. Without the mordant the dye would wash out of both cell types.`},{id:`q-pm-basics-staining-3`,topicId:`pm-basics-staining`,level:`intermediate`,difficulty:`medium`,stem:`The decolourising agent used in the Ziehl-Neelsen stain for Mycobacterium tuberculosis is`,options:[`95 percent ethanol alone`,`Acetone`,`1 percent sodium hydroxide`,`20 percent aqueous sulphuric acid`],answer:3,explanation:`Acid-fast bacilli resist decolourisation by strong acid because of the mycolic acid in their wall, so classical Ziehl-Neelsen uses 20 percent aqueous sulphuric acid; M. leprae is decolourised with a milder 5 percent acid. Do not confuse this with acid-alcohol (about 3 percent hydrochloric acid in 95 percent ethanol), which is a different reagent used in some modified protocols. Plain 95 percent ethanol is the Gram decolouriser and would not distinguish acid-fast organisms. Methylene blue then counterstains the non-acid-fast background blue.`},{id:`q-pm-basics-staining-4`,topicId:`pm-basics-staining`,level:`intermediate`,difficulty:`medium`,stem:`Teichoic acid is a characteristic component of the cell wall of`,options:[`Gram positive bacteria`,`Gram negative bacteria`,`Mycoplasma`,`Fungi`],answer:0,explanation:`Teichoic and lipoteichoic acids are polymers of ribitol or glycerol phosphate found only in the thick peptidoglycan of Gram positive walls, where they contribute the negative surface charge. Gram negatives instead have an outer membrane containing lipopolysaccharide. Mycoplasma has no wall at all.`},{id:`q-pm-basics-staining-5`,topicId:`pm-basics-staining`,level:`intermediate`,difficulty:`medium`,stem:`Endospore formation and most secondary metabolite production occur in which phase of the bacterial growth curve?`,options:[`Lag phase`,`Log phase`,`Stationary phase`,`Decline phase`],answer:2,explanation:`In the stationary phase nutrients are exhausted and toxic products accumulate, which triggers sporulation and switches on secondary metabolism (the idiophase). The log phase is when growth rate is maximal and cells are metabolically uniform, which is why it is used for antibiotic susceptibility testing but not for antibiotic production.`},{id:`q-pm-basics-staining-6`,topicId:`pm-basics-staining`,level:`intermediate`,difficulty:`medium`,stem:`Selenite F broth used for stool samples is best classified as a`,options:[`Differential medium`,`Transport medium`,`Enrichment medium`,`Enriched medium`],answer:2,explanation:`Selenite F is a liquid medium that suppresses coliforms and allows Salmonella to multiply preferentially, which is the definition of an enrichment medium. An enriched medium (blood or chocolate agar) simply adds nutrients for fastidious organisms and inhibits nothing, so the similar-sounding option is the trap. Selective media do the same job as enrichment media but are solid.`,tag:`Frequently asked`},{id:`q-pm-basics-staining-7`,topicId:`pm-basics-staining`,level:`advanced`,difficulty:`hard`,stem:`A culture grows from 10^2 to 10^8 cells per mL in 10 hours. The generation time is`,options:[`20 minutes`,`30 minutes`,`45 minutes`,`60 minutes`],answer:1,explanation:`Number of generations n = (log Nt - log N0)/log 2 = (8 - 2)/0.301 = 19.93, that is about 20 generations. Generation time g = t/n = 600 min / 20 = 30 minutes. A common error is to divide 600 by 6 (the number of log cycles) and answer 100 min, forgetting that each generation is a doubling, not a tenfold rise.`,tag:`Numerical`},{id:`q-pm-basics-staining-8`,topicId:`pm-basics-staining`,level:`advanced`,difficulty:`hard`,stem:`Lysozyme lyses bacteria by hydrolysing`,options:[`The peptide cross-bridge between D-alanine and glycine`,`The beta-1,4 bond between N-acetylmuramic acid and N-acetylglucosamine`,`The alpha-1,4 bond between glucose units`,`The ester bond in lipid A`],answer:1,explanation:`Lysozyme is a muramidase: it cuts the beta-1,4 glycosidic bond of the glycan backbone between NAM and NAG, destroying the wall directly. Penicillin, by contrast, blocks the transpeptidase that forms the peptide cross-link, which is the tempting first option. Gram positives are far more sensitive to lysozyme because their peptidoglycan is exposed, while the Gram negative outer membrane shields it.`},{id:`q-pm-sterilisation-1`,topicId:`pm-sterilisation`,level:`basic`,difficulty:`easy`,stem:`The standard autoclave cycle for sterilisation is`,options:[`121 degrees C at 15 psi for 15 min`,`115 degrees C at 10 psi for 15 min`,`134 degrees C at 15 psi for 30 min`,`160 degrees C at 15 psi for 60 min`],answer:0,explanation:`The reference moist heat cycle is 121 degrees C at 15 psi gauge pressure for a 15 minute holding time. 115 degrees C at 10 psi is also official but requires 30 minutes, and 134 degrees C needs 32 psi for 3 minutes. 160 degrees C for 1 hour belongs to the dry heat oven, where pressure is irrelevant.`,tag:`Frequently asked`},{id:`q-pm-sterilisation-2`,topicId:`pm-sterilisation`,level:`basic`,difficulty:`easy`,stem:`Sterilisation in a hot air oven at 160 degrees C requires a holding time of`,options:[`15 minutes`,`30 minutes`,`60 minutes`,`120 minutes`],answer:3,explanation:`Learn the set as a ladder: 160 degrees C for 120 min, 170 degrees C for 60 min, 180 degrees C for 30 min — every 10 degrees halves the time. This is the combination given in the IP and in the Indian microbiology texts, and it is what GPAT expects. Be aware that some non-Indian sources quote 160 degrees C for only 60 min, so a foreign textbook may appear to contradict this; go with the ladder above. Dry heat kills by oxidation rather than protein coagulation, which is why it needs both a higher temperature and far longer than moist heat.`},{id:`q-pm-sterilisation-3`,topicId:`pm-sterilisation`,level:`intermediate`,difficulty:`medium`,stem:`The D value of a sterilisation process is defined as the time required to`,options:[`Kill the entire population`,`Reduce the population by 90 percent`,`Reduce the D value tenfold`,`Raise the temperature by 10 degrees C`],answer:1,explanation:`The D (decimal reduction) value is the time at a stated temperature that reduces the viable count by one logarithm, that is by 90 percent. Reducing the D value tenfold defines the Z value, which is a temperature interval and not a time, so that is the standard distractor. Complete kill can never be defined in finite time because inactivation is logarithmic.`},{id:`q-pm-sterilisation-4`,topicId:`pm-sterilisation`,level:`intermediate`,difficulty:`medium`,stem:`A product is held at 115 degrees C for 30 minutes. Taking Z as 10 degrees C, the F0 delivered is approximately`,options:[`3.0 minutes`,`7.5 minutes`,`30 minutes`,`120 minutes`],answer:1,explanation:`F0 = t x 10^((T - 121)/Z) = 30 x 10^((115 - 121)/10) = 30 x 10^-0.6 = 30 x 0.251 = 7.5 minutes. Because the product ran 6 degrees below 121 degrees C, its 30 real minutes are worth only 7.5 equivalent minutes, which is just below the pharmacopoeial minimum F0 of 8. Answering 30 minutes ignores the temperature correction entirely.`,tag:`Numerical`},{id:`q-pm-sterilisation-5`,topicId:`pm-sterilisation`,level:`intermediate`,difficulty:`medium`,stem:`The challenge organism used to validate a 0.22 micrometre sterilising grade membrane filter is`,options:[`Geobacillus stearothermophilus`,`Bacillus atrophaeus`,`Brevundimonas diminuta`,`Acholeplasma laidlawii`],answer:2,explanation:`Brevundimonas (formerly Pseudomonas) diminuta is the smallest routinely encountered bacterium and is used at 10^7 organisms per square centimetre of filter area to prove full retention by a 0.22 micrometre membrane. Acholeplasma laidlawii is the corresponding challenge for a 0.1 micrometre mycoplasma-retentive filter. The two Bacillus species are heat and gas sterilisation indicators, not filter challenges.`},{id:`q-pm-sterilisation-6`,topicId:`pm-sterilisation`,level:`intermediate`,difficulty:`medium`,stem:`Ethylene oxide is diluted with carbon dioxide or nitrogen before use mainly because it is`,options:[`Explosive above about 3 percent in air`,`Inactive when pure`,`Too rapidly hydrolysed`,`Corrosive to stainless steel`],answer:0,explanation:`Pure ethylene oxide forms an explosive mixture with air above roughly 3 percent by volume, so it is supplied blended with an inert carrier gas. It is fully active when pure, and the reason for its other operating requirement, 30 to 70 percent relative humidity, is that moisture is needed for alkylation. Its main safety concerns after flammability are mutagenicity and the residues ethylene chlorohydrin and ethylene glycol.`,tag:`Frequently asked`},{id:`q-pm-sterilisation-7`,topicId:`pm-sterilisation`,level:`advanced`,difficulty:`hard`,stem:`A solution carries a bioburden of 10^6 organisms per container with a D121 of 1.5 minutes. The exposure time at 121 degrees C needed to reach a sterility assurance level of 10^-6 is`,options:[`9 minutes`,`15 minutes`,`18 minutes`,`24 minutes`],answer:2,explanation:`The required log reduction is from 10^6 down to 10^-6, that is 6 - (-6) = 12 logs. Time = D x log reduction = 1.5 x 12 = 18 minutes. Answering 9 minutes counts only the 6 logs needed to reach one survivor and forgets the further 6 logs of safety margin that define the 10^-6 SAL.`,tag:`Numerical`},{id:`q-pm-sterilisation-8`,topicId:`pm-sterilisation`,level:`advanced`,difficulty:`hard`,stem:`For a spore preparation the D value at 110 degrees C is 20 minutes and at 120 degrees C is 2 minutes. The Z value is`,options:[`2 degrees C`,`10 degrees C`,`18 degrees C`,`20 degrees C`],answer:1,explanation:`Z is the temperature rise that divides the D value by ten. Here a 10 degree rise took D from 20 to 2 minutes, exactly one log, so Z = 10 degrees C. Using the equation log D1 - log D2 = (T2 - T1)/Z gives (1.301 - 0.301) = 10/Z, hence Z = 10. Choosing 20 confuses the Z value with the D value at the lower temperature.`,tag:`Numerical`},{id:`q-pm-sterility-validation-1`,topicId:`pm-sterility-validation`,level:`basic`,difficulty:`easy`,stem:`The two media officially prescribed for the sterility test are`,options:[`Nutrient broth and MacConkey broth`,`Blood agar and chocolate agar`,`Sabouraud dextrose broth and selenite F broth`,`Fluid thioglycollate medium and soybean casein digest medium`],answer:3,explanation:`Fluid thioglycollate medium supports anaerobes and aerobes at 30 to 35 degrees C, while soybean casein digest medium (tryptone soya broth) supports fungi and aerobic bacteria at 20 to 25 degrees C. Between them they cover the whole range of likely contaminants. Sabouraud broth is a general fungal medium but is not one of the two official sterility test media.`,tag:`Frequently asked`},{id:`q-pm-sterility-validation-2`,topicId:`pm-sterility-validation`,level:`basic`,difficulty:`easy`,stem:`Soybean casein digest medium in the sterility test is incubated at`,options:[`20 to 25 degrees C`,`30 to 35 degrees C`,`37 degrees C exactly`,`42 to 45 degrees C`],answer:0,explanation:`Soybean casein digest medium is held at 20 to 25 degrees C because that range favours fungi and moulds, which grow poorly at body temperature. Fluid thioglycollate medium is the one incubated at 30 to 35 degrees C for bacteria. Both are read over a 14 day incubation.`},{id:`q-pm-sterility-validation-3`,topicId:`pm-sterility-validation`,level:`intermediate`,difficulty:`medium`,stem:`For a filterable injection that itself has antimicrobial activity, the preferred sterility testing method is`,options:[`Direct inoculation into a larger volume of medium`,`Direct inoculation after heating the sample`,`Pour plate counting`,`Membrane filtration with rinsing of the membrane`],answer:3,explanation:`Membrane filtration traps any organisms on the membrane while the antimicrobial passes through and is then washed away with rinse fluid, so inhibition is eliminated rather than merely diluted. Direct inoculation only dilutes the inhibitor, and with a potent antimicrobial the dilution may still leave inhibitory concentrations. Pour plating is a counting technique, not a sterility test.`},{id:`q-pm-sterility-validation-4`,topicId:`pm-sterility-validation`,level:`intermediate`,difficulty:`medium`,stem:`The nominal pore size of the membrane used in the sterility test by membrane filtration is`,options:[`0.1 micrometre`,`0.22 micrometre`,`0.45 micrometre`,`1.2 micrometre`],answer:2,explanation:`The sterility test membrane is 0.45 micrometre, since it only has to retain any contaminant present and the larger pore filters viscous products faster. The 0.22 micrometre rating belongs to sterilising grade filtration of the product itself, which is the classic confusion in this question. A 0.1 micrometre membrane is reserved for mycoplasma retention.`,tag:`Frequently asked`},{id:`q-pm-sterility-validation-5`,topicId:`pm-sterility-validation`,level:`intermediate`,difficulty:`medium`,stem:`The biological indicator organism used to validate ethylene oxide sterilisation is`,options:[`Bacillus atrophaeus`,`Geobacillus stearothermophilus`,`Bacillus pumilus`,`Clostridium sporogenes`],answer:0,explanation:`Bacillus atrophaeus (formerly Bacillus subtilis var. niger) spores are the indicator for both ethylene oxide and dry heat. Geobacillus stearothermophilus is the moist heat indicator and Bacillus pumilus is used for ionising radiation. Clostridium sporogenes is used as a growth promotion control organism for anaerobic media, not as a sterilisation indicator.`},{id:`q-pm-sterility-validation-6`,topicId:`pm-sterility-validation`,level:`intermediate`,difficulty:`medium`,stem:`EU GMP Grade A, the zone used for aseptic filling, corresponds to which ISO 14644 classification and recommended air velocity?`,options:[`ISO 8 and 0.1 metre per second`,`ISO 7 and 0.25 metre per second`,`ISO 5 and about 0.45 metre per second`,`ISO 3 and 1.0 metre per second`],answer:2,explanation:`Grade A equals ISO 5 (the old Class 100), with unidirectional airflow of 0.36 to 0.54 metre per second, usually quoted as 0.45. Its microbial limit is less than 1 cfu per cubic metre. ISO 7 and ISO 8 correspond to Grades C and D, the supporting areas, which need at least 20 air changes per hour but not unidirectional flow.`},{id:`q-pm-sterility-validation-7`,topicId:`pm-sterility-validation`,level:`advanced`,difficulty:`hard`,stem:`A parenteral drug has a maximum human dose of 10 mg per kg per hour. Taking K as 5.0 EU per kg per hour, the bacterial endotoxin limit for the product is`,options:[`0.5 EU per mg`,`2.0 EU per mg`,`5.0 EU per mg`,`50 EU per mg`],answer:0,explanation:`The endotoxin limit is K/M = 5.0 EU per kg per hour divided by 10 mg per kg per hour = 0.5 EU per mg. Note that the limit falls as the dose rises, because a bigger dose delivers more endotoxin per patient. For the intrathecal route K drops to 0.2 EU per kg, giving a limit twenty-five times stricter.`,tag:`Numerical`},{id:`q-pm-sterility-validation-8`,topicId:`pm-sterility-validation`,level:`advanced`,difficulty:`hard`,stem:`Which statement about pyrogens in a parenteral product is correct?`,options:[`They are destroyed by autoclaving at 121 degrees C for 15 min`,`They are removed by a 0.22 micrometre sterilising filter`,`They are destroyed by dry heat at 250 degrees C for 30 min`,`They are always removed along with the bacteria that produced them`],answer:2,explanation:`Bacterial endotoxin is a heat-stable lipopolysaccharide, so depyrogenation of glass and metal needs dry heat at about 250 degrees C for 30 minutes. A normal autoclave cycle sterilises without depyrogenating, and endotoxin molecules are far smaller than a 0.22 micrometre pore, so they pass straight through a sterilising filter. A solution can therefore be perfectly sterile and still pyrogenic.`,tag:`Frequently asked`},{id:`q-pm-microbial-assay-1`,topicId:`pm-microbial-assay`,level:`basic`,difficulty:`easy`,stem:`In the cylinder-plate microbial assay, the diameter of the zone of inhibition is proportional to`,options:[`The concentration of the antibiotic`,`The logarithm of the concentration of the antibiotic`,`The square of the concentration`,`The reciprocal of the concentration`],answer:1,explanation:`Because the drug diffuses radially from the cylinder, zone diameter plotted against log dose gives a straight line, and this is the basis of the parallel line assay used to calculate potency. A plot against concentration itself is curved, so choosing plain concentration rather than its logarithm is the standard error here. The response is a growth-inhibition response, so bigger zones mean more antibiotic.`,tag:`Frequently asked`},{id:`q-pm-microbial-assay-2`,topicId:`pm-microbial-assay`,level:`basic`,difficulty:`easy`,stem:`The organism used in the microbial assay of cyanocobalamin (vitamin B12) is`,options:[`Staphylococcus aureus`,`Bacillus subtilis`,`Lactobacillus leichmannii`,`Saccharomyces cerevisiae`],answer:2,explanation:`Lactobacillus leichmannii cannot synthesise vitamin B12, so its growth in a B12-free basal medium is proportional to the vitamin supplied. Staphylococcus aureus and Bacillus subtilis are used for penicillin and streptomycin assays respectively, and Saccharomyces cerevisiae for the polyene antifungals. Vitamin assays are growth-promotion assays, unlike antibiotic assays.`},{id:`q-pm-microbial-assay-3`,topicId:`pm-microbial-assay`,level:`intermediate`,difficulty:`medium`,stem:`In the turbidimetric assay of an antibiotic, the tubes are typically incubated for`,options:[`3 to 4 hours and read as absorbance`,`16 to 18 hours and read as zone diameter`,`24 hours and read visually`,`48 hours and read by plate count`],answer:0,explanation:`The turbidimetric method needs only 3 to 4 hours at 37 degrees C because it measures partial inhibition of growth in liquid medium as absorbance near 530 nm, which is why it is chosen when a same-day result is needed. Zone diameter after 16 to 18 hours belongs to the cylinder-plate method. The trade-off is that turbid or coloured samples cannot be assayed turbidimetrically.`},{id:`q-pm-microbial-assay-4`,topicId:`pm-microbial-assay`,level:`intermediate`,difficulty:`medium`,stem:`A crude antibiotic preparation is deeply coloured and slightly turbid. The appropriate assay method is`,options:[`Turbidimetric tube assay`,`Direct UV absorbance at 280 nm`,`Nephelometry`,`Cylinder-plate agar diffusion assay`],answer:3,explanation:`Turbidimetry and nephelometry both read light scattering or absorbance, so intrinsic colour and turbidity give a false growth reading. The cylinder-plate method measures a zone diameter, which is unaffected by the colour of the sample. UV absorbance is a chemical measurement and would not give biological potency at all.`},{id:`q-pm-microbial-assay-5`,topicId:`pm-microbial-assay`,level:`intermediate`,difficulty:`medium`,stem:`The minimum bactericidal concentration is defined as the lowest concentration that`,options:[`Kills 99.9 percent of the original inoculum`,`Prevents visible growth in broth after 18 to 24 h`,`Kills 50 percent of the inoculum`,`Produces a measurable zone of inhibition`],answer:0,explanation:`MBC is determined by subculturing the clear tubes of an MIC series onto drug-free agar and finding the lowest concentration giving a 99.9 percent (3 log) kill. Prevention of visible growth defines the MIC, which is the tempting distractor, and MBC is always equal to or greater than MIC. An MBC to MIC ratio above 4 indicates tolerance.`},{id:`q-pm-microbial-assay-6`,topicId:`pm-microbial-assay`,level:`intermediate`,difficulty:`medium`,stem:`One international unit of benzylpenicillin corresponds to`,options:[`0.1 microgram of the sodium salt`,`0.6 microgram of the sodium salt`,`1.0 microgram of the sodium salt`,`6.0 micrograms of the sodium salt`],answer:1,explanation:`One IU of penicillin G equals 0.6 microgram of the sodium salt, so 1 mg is about 1667 IU and 1 million units is about 600 mg. Antibiotic potency is expressed in units precisely because biological activity, not chemical content, is what matters. Always check whether a stated potency refers to the free acid or to a salt.`,tag:`Numerical`},{id:`q-pm-microbial-assay-7`,topicId:`pm-microbial-assay`,level:`advanced`,difficulty:`hard`,stem:`In an agar diffusion assay, increasing the inoculum density of the test organism in the seed layer will`,options:[`Increase the zone diameter`,`Decrease the zone diameter`,`Have no effect on the zone diameter`,`Make the zone edge sharper without changing its size`],answer:1,explanation:`A heavier inoculum reaches visible growth sooner, so the drug has less time to diffuse before the growth front is fixed, and the zone is smaller. This is why inoculum density must be standardised: an over-inoculated plate makes a normal sample read as sub-potent. Larger zones are instead obtained with a thinner seed layer or a pre-diffusion period at 4 degrees C.`},{id:`q-pm-microbial-assay-8`,topicId:`pm-microbial-assay`,level:`advanced`,difficulty:`hard`,stem:`For an organism the MIC of a drug is 2 micrograms per mL and the MBC is 16 micrograms per mL. This indicates that the organism is`,options:[`Fully susceptible and the drug is bactericidal`,`Resistant, since the MIC is above 1 microgram per mL`,`Intrinsically resistant to all beta-lactams`,`Tolerant, since the MBC to MIC ratio exceeds 4`],answer:3,explanation:`The MBC to MIC ratio is 16/2 = 8. A ratio of 4 or less means the drug kills at close to the inhibitory concentration and is bactericidal; a ratio above 4 defines tolerance, where growth is inhibited but the organism is not killed. Resistance is judged against clinical breakpoints, not against an arbitrary 1 microgram per mL cut-off.`,tag:`Numerical`},{id:`q-pm-immunology-vaccines-1`,topicId:`pm-immunology-vaccines`,level:`basic`,difficulty:`easy`,stem:`The only immunoglobulin class that crosses the human placenta is`,options:[`IgA`,`IgM`,`IgG`,`IgE`],answer:2,explanation:`IgG is transported across the placenta by the FcRn receptor and gives the newborn passive protection for the first few months. IgA is the secretory antibody passed in colostrum, not across the placenta, which is the common confusion. IgM is a pentamer and far too large to cross.`,tag:`Frequently asked`},{id:`q-pm-immunology-vaccines-2`,topicId:`pm-immunology-vaccines`,level:`basic`,difficulty:`easy`,stem:`The first antibody produced in a primary immune response, and the most efficient activator of complement, is`,options:[`IgM`,`IgG`,`IgD`,`IgE`],answer:0,explanation:`IgM appears first and is the best complement fixer because a single pentamer presents several adjacent Fc regions to C1q. IgG dominates only in the secondary response and is a weaker complement activator per molecule. Detecting specific IgM therefore indicates a recent or current infection.`},{id:`q-pm-immunology-vaccines-3`,topicId:`pm-immunology-vaccines`,level:`intermediate`,difficulty:`medium`,stem:`A hapten is best described as a substance that is`,options:[`Immunogenic but not antigenic`,`Antigenic but not immunogenic unless coupled to a carrier`,`Both immunogenic and antigenic on its own`,`Neither antigenic nor immunogenic`],answer:1,explanation:`A hapten is too small to provoke a response by itself, but once conjugated to a carrier protein it induces antibodies that then bind the free hapten, so it has antigenicity without independent immunogenicity. Penicillin is the classic example, its penicilloyl group binding serum protein lysine residues. Nothing can be immunogenic without also being antigenic, which rules out the first option.`},{id:`q-pm-immunology-vaccines-4`,topicId:`pm-immunology-vaccines`,level:`intermediate`,difficulty:`medium`,stem:`Serum sickness following administration of horse antitoxin is an example of which hypersensitivity reaction?`,options:[`Type I`,`Type II`,`Type III`,`Type IV`],answer:2,explanation:`Serum sickness is caused by circulating antigen-antibody complexes that deposit in vessel walls, fix complement and attract neutrophils, which is the definition of a type III reaction. Type I is the IgE-mediated immediate anaphylaxis that can also follow antiserum but appears within minutes. Type II is directed against antigens on cell surfaces, as in a mismatched transfusion.`},{id:`q-pm-immunology-vaccines-5`,topicId:`pm-immunology-vaccines`,level:`intermediate`,difficulty:`medium`,stem:`The tuberculin (Mantoux) reaction read at 48 to 72 hours is mediated by`,options:[`IgE bound to mast cells`,`IgG and complement acting on cell surfaces`,`Circulating immune complexes`,`Sensitised T lymphocytes and macrophages`],answer:3,explanation:`The tuberculin response is the standard type IV delayed hypersensitivity reaction: sensitised CD4 T cells release cytokines that recruit and activate macrophages, producing induration over 24 to 72 hours. No antibody is involved, so the reaction can be transferred by lymphocytes but not by serum. The delayed timing is itself the clue to the mechanism.`,tag:`Frequently asked`},{id:`q-pm-immunology-vaccines-6`,topicId:`pm-immunology-vaccines`,level:`intermediate`,difficulty:`medium`,stem:`Which of the following is a toxoid vaccine?`,options:[`Tetanus vaccine`,`BCG vaccine`,`Hepatitis B vaccine`,`Oral polio vaccine`],answer:0,explanation:`Tetanus and diphtheria vaccines are toxoids, exotoxins detoxified with formaldehyde so that they retain antigenicity but lose toxicity. BCG and oral polio are live attenuated vaccines, and hepatitis B vaccine is a recombinant subunit vaccine made of surface antigen. Toxoids are adsorbed on aluminium salts and therefore must never be frozen.`},{id:`q-pm-immunology-vaccines-7`,topicId:`pm-immunology-vaccines`,level:`advanced`,difficulty:`hard`,stem:`Digestion of an IgG molecule with pepsin yields`,options:[`Two Fab fragments and one Fc fragment`,`Two heavy chains and two light chains`,`One Fab fragment and two Fc fragments`,`One bivalent F(ab)2 fragment plus small Fc peptides`],answer:3,explanation:`Pepsin cleaves below the hinge disulphide bonds, so the two antigen-binding arms stay joined as a single bivalent F(ab)2 fragment while the Fc is degraded to small peptides. Papain cleaves above the hinge and gives two univalent Fab fragments plus an intact Fc, which is the classic distractor. Separating the chains themselves requires reduction of disulphide bonds, not proteolysis.`},{id:`q-pm-immunology-vaccines-8`,topicId:`pm-immunology-vaccines`,level:`advanced`,difficulty:`hard`,stem:`Conjugating a bacterial capsular polysaccharide to a carrier protein improves the vaccine mainly because it`,options:[`Converts a T-independent response into a T-dependent one with memory`,`Increases the molecular weight so the antigen is retained longer`,`Makes the polysaccharide resistant to enzymatic degradation`,`Allows the vaccine to be given orally`],answer:0,explanation:`Pure polysaccharides stimulate B cells without T cell help, giving mainly IgM, no affinity maturation and no memory, and they respond poorly in children under two years. Linking to a protein carrier brings in helper T cells, so the response class-switches to IgG, generates memory and works in infants, which is why Hib and pneumococcal conjugate vaccines are used in infant schedules. Molecular size alone does not explain the qualitative change in the response.`},{id:`q-pm-fermentation-1`,topicId:`pm-fermentation`,level:`basic`,difficulty:`easy`,stem:`Industrial production of penicillin uses`,options:[`Streptomyces griseus`,`Aspergillus niger`,`Propionibacterium shermanii`,`Penicillium chrysogenum`],answer:3,explanation:`Penicillium chrysogenum is the high-yielding species used industrially; Flemings original mould was P. notatum. Streptomyces griseus makes streptomycin, Aspergillus niger makes citric acid and Propionibacterium shermanii makes vitamin B12. Each of these organism-product pairs is asked routinely.`,tag:`Frequently asked`},{id:`q-pm-fermentation-2`,topicId:`pm-fermentation`,level:`basic`,difficulty:`easy`,stem:`The precursor added to the fermentation medium to direct synthesis towards penicillin G is`,options:[`Phenoxyacetic acid`,`Corn steep liquor`,`Phenylacetic acid`,`Lactose`],answer:2,explanation:`Phenylacetic acid supplies the benzyl side chain of penicillin G, while phenoxyacetic acid gives penicillin V, so the two must not be interchanged. Corn steep liquor is the nitrogen-rich medium base and lactose the slowly used carbon source; neither is a precursor in the strict sense.`},{id:`q-pm-fermentation-3`,topicId:`pm-fermentation`,level:`intermediate`,difficulty:`medium`,stem:`In the production of citric acid by Aspergillus niger, potassium ferrocyanide is added to the molasses medium in order to`,options:[`Act as a buffer at pH 2`,`Precipitate excess iron and other trace metals`,`Serve as a nitrogen source`,`Prevent foaming`],answer:1,explanation:`Citric acid accumulates only when trace metals, especially iron and manganese, are deficient, and potassium ferrocyanide is added to molasses to precipitate excess iron. If iron is left high the tricarboxylic acid cycle runs to completion and yields collapse. The low pH near 2 is maintained separately and serves to suppress oxalic and gluconic acid formation.`,tag:`Frequently asked`},{id:`q-pm-fermentation-4`,topicId:`pm-fermentation`,level:`intermediate`,difficulty:`medium`,stem:`Antibiotics are formed during which phase of a fermentation?`,options:[`Trophophase, alongside growth`,`Idiophase, after growth has slowed`,`Lag phase, before growth begins`,`Only during the death phase`],answer:1,explanation:`Antibiotics are secondary metabolites produced in the idiophase, when growth slows and the cell no longer needs its full primary metabolism. Primary metabolites such as ethanol, lactic acid and amino acids are made in the trophophase and are growth associated. Citric acid is the classic exception, a primary metabolite made by a mould.`},{id:`q-pm-fermentation-5`,topicId:`pm-fermentation`,level:`intermediate`,difficulty:`medium`,stem:`Baffles are fitted inside a stirred tank fermenter mainly to`,options:[`Prevent vortex formation and improve mixing`,`Increase the working volume`,`Support the sparger`,`Act as heat transfer surfaces only`],answer:0,explanation:`Four vertical baffles of about one tenth of the vessel diameter break the rotational flow generated by the impeller, preventing a vortex and converting the motion into top-to-bottom turbulence that raises oxygen transfer. They may incidentally carry cooling coils, but that is not their primary purpose. Without baffles the liquid rotates as a solid body and mixing effectively stops.`},{id:`q-pm-fermentation-6`,topicId:`pm-fermentation`,level:`intermediate`,difficulty:`medium`,stem:`Cobalt chloride is added to the fermentation medium for vitamin B12 because cobalt`,options:[`Acts as an antifoam agent`,`Inhibits contaminating organisms`,`Is the central metal atom of the corrin ring`,`Buffers the medium at pH 7`],answer:2,explanation:`Cyanocobalamin contains a cobalt atom coordinated at the centre of its corrin ring, so the metal must be supplied in the medium; 5,6-dimethylbenzimidazole is added as the precursor of the nucleotide base. Cobalt has no antifoam or buffering role here. The vitamin is intracellular, so cells are harvested and extracted rather than the broth being processed directly.`},{id:`q-pm-fermentation-7`,topicId:`pm-fermentation`,level:`advanced`,difficulty:`hard`,stem:`An organism has a maximum specific growth rate of 0.4 per hour and a Monod constant Ks of 0.5 g per litre. At a limiting substrate concentration of 1.5 g per litre the specific growth rate is`,options:[`0.10 per hour`,`0.30 per hour`,`0.40 per hour`,`0.53 per hour`],answer:1,explanation:`Using mu = mu(max) x S/(Ks + S) = 0.4 x 1.5/(0.5 + 1.5) = 0.4 x 0.75 = 0.30 per hour. Answering 0.40 assumes the substrate is saturating, but S is only three times Ks, so growth is still substrate limited. Note that mu can approach but never exceed mu(max), which rules out 0.53.`,tag:`Numerical`},{id:`q-pm-fermentation-8`,topicId:`pm-fermentation`,level:`advanced`,difficulty:`hard`,stem:`Streptomycin is recovered from the clarified fermentation broth mainly by`,options:[`Solvent extraction into amyl acetate at pH 2`,`Steam distillation`,`Precipitation as calcium salt with lime`,`Adsorption on a cation exchange resin followed by elution`],answer:3,explanation:`Streptomycin is a strongly basic, highly water-soluble aminoglycoside, so it stays in the aqueous phase and is captured on a weakly acidic cation exchange resin or activated charcoal, then eluted and precipitated as the sulphate. Solvent extraction into amyl acetate at low pH is the penicillin route and would not work for a permanently charged base. Lime precipitation belongs to citric acid recovery.`}],n=[{id:`f-pm-basics-staining-1`,topicId:`pm-basics-staining`,front:`Gram stain reagents in order?`,back:`Crystal violet, Grams iodine (mordant), alcohol or acetone (decolouriser), safranin (counterstain).`},{id:`f-pm-basics-staining-2`,topicId:`pm-basics-staining`,front:`Ziehl-Neelsen reagents in order?`,back:`Carbol fuchsin with heat, 20 percent sulphuric acid (acid-alcohol), methylene blue. Acid-fast bacilli appear red.`},{id:`f-pm-basics-staining-3`,topicId:`pm-basics-staining`,front:`Ribosome and cell wall polymer of a prokaryote?`,back:`70S ribosome (50S + 30S); cell wall of peptidoglycan (murein).`},{id:`f-pm-basics-staining-4`,topicId:`pm-basics-staining`,front:`Generation time formula?`,back:`n = (log Nt - log N0)/log 2 = 3.3 (log Nt - log N0), and g = t/n.`},{id:`f-pm-basics-staining-5`,topicId:`pm-basics-staining`,front:`Four phases of the bacterial growth curve?`,back:`Lag, log (exponential), stationary, decline (death).`},{id:`f-pm-basics-staining-6`,topicId:`pm-basics-staining`,front:`What makes the endospore heat resistant?`,back:`Calcium dipicolinate plus a dehydrated core, protected by cortex and keratin-like spore coat.`},{id:`f-pm-sterilisation-1`,topicId:`pm-sterilisation`,front:`Standard autoclave cycle?`,back:`121 degrees C, 15 psi, 15 minutes of saturated steam.`},{id:`f-pm-sterilisation-2`,topicId:`pm-sterilisation`,front:`Dry heat time-temperature combinations?`,back:`160 degrees C for 120 min, 170 degrees C for 60 min, 180 degrees C for 30 min. Depyrogenation at 250 degrees C for 30 min.`},{id:`f-pm-sterilisation-3`,topicId:`pm-sterilisation`,front:`Define D, Z and F0.`,back:`D = min for a 90 percent kill at a stated temperature. Z = degrees C rise that cuts D tenfold (10 for steam). F0 = equivalent minutes at 121 degrees C with Z = 10.`},{id:`f-pm-sterilisation-4`,topicId:`pm-sterilisation`,front:`Standard gamma sterilisation dose and source?`,back:`25 kGy (2.5 Mrad) from cobalt-60.`},{id:`f-pm-sterilisation-5`,topicId:`pm-sterilisation`,front:`Ethylene oxide operating conditions?`,back:`800 to 1200 mg per litre, 45 to 63 degrees C, 30 to 70 percent relative humidity, several hours, then aeration. Explosive above 3 percent in air.`},{id:`f-pm-sterilisation-6`,topicId:`pm-sterilisation`,front:`What does a 0.22 micrometre filter not remove?`,back:`Viruses, mycoplasma (reliably) and pyrogens.`},{id:`f-pm-sterility-validation-1`,topicId:`pm-sterility-validation`,front:`Two sterility test media, temperatures and targets?`,back:`Fluid thioglycollate, 30 to 35 degrees C, anaerobes and aerobes. Soybean casein digest, 20 to 25 degrees C, fungi and aerobes. Both for 14 days.`},{id:`f-pm-sterility-validation-2`,topicId:`pm-sterility-validation`,front:`Membrane pore size used in the sterility test?`,back:`0.45 micrometre (not 0.22, which is for sterilising filtration).`},{id:`f-pm-sterility-validation-3`,topicId:`pm-sterility-validation`,front:`Biological indicators for moist heat, dry heat or EtO, and radiation?`,back:`Geobacillus stearothermophilus; Bacillus atrophaeus; Bacillus pumilus.`},{id:`f-pm-sterility-validation-4`,topicId:`pm-sterility-validation`,front:`Clean room grades A to D as ISO classes?`,back:`A and B = ISO 5 (Class 100), C = ISO 7 (Class 10 000), D = ISO 8 (Class 100 000). At least 20 air changes per hour in B, C, D.`},{id:`f-pm-sterility-validation-5`,topicId:`pm-sterility-validation`,front:`Endotoxin limit equation?`,back:`Limit = K/M, with K = 5.0 EU per kg per hour for parenterals and 0.2 EU per kg for intrathecal; M = maximum dose per kg per hour.`},{id:`f-pm-sterility-validation-6`,topicId:`pm-sterility-validation`,front:`Media fill acceptance criterion?`,back:`Contamination rate below 0.1 percent; with 5000 or more units filled, not more than one contaminated unit.`},{id:`f-pm-microbial-assay-1`,topicId:`pm-microbial-assay`,front:`Two official methods of antibiotic microbial assay?`,back:`Cylinder-plate (agar diffusion, zone diameter) and turbidimetric (tube assay, absorbance near 530 nm).`},{id:`f-pm-microbial-assay-2`,topicId:`pm-microbial-assay`,front:`Zone diameter is proportional to what?`,back:`The logarithm of the antibiotic concentration, giving a straight line on a log-dose plot.`},{id:`f-pm-microbial-assay-3`,topicId:`pm-microbial-assay`,front:`Test organisms: penicillin, streptomycin, vitamin B12?`,back:`Staphylococcus aureus; Bacillus subtilis; Lactobacillus leichmannii.`},{id:`f-pm-microbial-assay-4`,topicId:`pm-microbial-assay`,front:`MIC versus MBC?`,back:`MIC = lowest concentration preventing visible growth in 18 to 24 h. MBC = lowest concentration killing 99.9 percent of the inoculum. Ratio above 4 means tolerance.`},{id:`f-pm-microbial-assay-5`,topicId:`pm-microbial-assay`,front:`One international unit of penicillin G equals?`,back:`0.6 microgram of the sodium salt, so 1 mg is about 1667 IU.`},{id:`f-pm-microbial-assay-6`,topicId:`pm-microbial-assay`,front:`When must the turbidimetric assay be avoided?`,back:`With coloured, turbid or particulate samples; use the cylinder-plate method instead.`},{id:`f-pm-immunology-vaccines-1`,topicId:`pm-immunology-vaccines`,front:`Immunoglobulin superlatives?`,back:`IgG most abundant and only one crossing the placenta; IgM largest and first formed; IgA in secretions; IgD B cell receptor; IgE least abundant, allergy.`},{id:`f-pm-immunology-vaccines-2`,topicId:`pm-immunology-vaccines`,front:`Four hypersensitivity types (ACID)?`,back:`I Anaphylactic (IgE); II Cytotoxic (IgG or IgM on cell surface); III Immune complex; IV Delayed (T cell).`},{id:`f-pm-immunology-vaccines-3`,topicId:`pm-immunology-vaccines`,front:`Papain versus pepsin digestion of IgG?`,back:`Papain gives 2 Fab + 1 Fc. Pepsin gives one bivalent F(ab)2 plus degraded Fc.`},{id:`f-pm-immunology-vaccines-4`,topicId:`pm-immunology-vaccines`,front:`Name two live attenuated and two toxoid vaccines.`,back:`Live: BCG, oral polio (also MMR, varicella). Toxoid: diphtheria and tetanus.`},{id:`f-pm-immunology-vaccines-5`,topicId:`pm-immunology-vaccines`,front:`Commonest human vaccine adjuvant?`,back:`Aluminium hydroxide or phosphate (alum). Freund complete adjuvant is animal use only.`},{id:`f-pm-immunology-vaccines-6`,topicId:`pm-immunology-vaccines`,front:`Primary versus secondary antibody response?`,back:`Primary: 5 to 10 day lag, low titre, mainly IgM. Secondary: 1 to 3 day lag, much higher titre, mainly high-affinity IgG.`},{id:`f-pm-fermentation-1`,topicId:`pm-fermentation`,front:`Organisms for penicillin, streptomycin, vitamin B12 and citric acid?`,back:`Penicillium chrysogenum; Streptomyces griseus; Propionibacterium shermanii or Pseudomonas denitrificans; Aspergillus niger.`},{id:`f-pm-fermentation-2`,topicId:`pm-fermentation`,front:`Precursors for penicillin G and penicillin V?`,back:`Phenylacetic acid for penicillin G; phenoxyacetic acid for penicillin V.`},{id:`f-pm-fermentation-3`,topicId:`pm-fermentation`,front:`Primary versus secondary metabolite?`,back:`Primary: made in the trophophase, needed for growth (ethanol, citric acid, amino acids). Secondary: made in the idiophase, not needed for growth (antibiotics, alkaloids).`},{id:`f-pm-fermentation-4`,topicId:`pm-fermentation`,front:`Order of downstream processing steps?`,back:`Cell separation, cell disruption if intracellular, primary isolation and concentration, purification by chromatography or crystallisation, then polishing and drying.`},{id:`f-pm-fermentation-5`,topicId:`pm-fermentation`,front:`Key control in citric acid fermentation?`,back:`Trace metal (iron and manganese) deficiency, achieved with potassium ferrocyanide, plus pH below about 2.`},{id:`f-pm-fermentation-6`,topicId:`pm-fermentation`,front:`Oxygen transfer rate equation in a fermenter?`,back:`OTR = kLa x (C* - CL), where kLa is the volumetric mass transfer coefficient and C* - CL the dissolved oxygen driving force.`}];export{n as flashcards,e as notes,t as questions};